1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,700
"Games as a Service".

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,200
If you've been involved with computer or video games

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,800
for any length of time

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,200
you've probably come across that phrase.

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,400
I don't know what the origin is.

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,400
Wikpedia's no help.

7
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:16,300
Look at this first sentence under 'History'.

8
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,100
"The idea of Games as a Service began around 2004

9
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,900
with the introduction of-" blah blah blah

10
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,200
that doesn't matter, that's already wrong.

11
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:28,700
I mean EverQuest was the first major game
I knew about doing this in '99

12
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:33,200
but even before that, I remember hearing
about the Sierra Network from '91.

13
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:37,900
I really doubt that was even when this started
either, it was probably some MUD.

14
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:42,100
Yeah, before World of Warcraft, there were MUDs,
fun for everyone.

15
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,700
"2004"...you're no help at all
here Wikipedia.

16
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:49,000
And I got sidetracked in the first minute.
Nice.

17
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,700
Anyway, what I was getting at is I don't
know the origin of this phrase,

18
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:55,200
but it's so thoroughly entered the vernacular,

19
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,300
that I'm honestly wondering if it's the result
of some subtle propaganda campaign.

20
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,600
I say that because it's such a harmless
sounding phrase

21
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:06,300
but it hides a terrible if not illegal
business practice.

22
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:08,200
And that bring me to the title:

23
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,100
"Games as a Service is Fraud".

24
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:14,400
Now that sounds clickbait-y, and,
I guess it is

25
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,800
but, I'm not exaggerating. I mean it.

26
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:21,641
Unfortunately, it's not the kind of fraud
I can easily explain in one sentence.

27
00:01:21,641 --> 00:01:24,241
So it's not like changing the odometer
on a car

28
00:01:24,241 --> 00:01:27,573
then covering it up and selling it
as though you never did that.

29
00:01:27,573 --> 00:01:30,600
That's easy fraud.
Anyone understands that.

30
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:33,700
This is a more sophisticated kind.

31
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:37,300
Like how even now, a lot of people
are still confused on how Goldman Sachs

32
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:40,400
broke the law leading up to the 2008
finanical crash

33
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:42,600
and how a lot of people should have gone to prison.

34
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,795
This is more like that.

35
00:01:44,795 --> 00:01:48,960
Now I'll warn you, this video is going to
be long, and could get boring in spots,

36
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,550
but, it's necessary.

37
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:55,400
See, this video is my declaration of war
on Games as a Service.

38
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,555
I'm going up against years of pushed
narrative here,

39
00:01:58,555 --> 00:02:00,055
this isn't gonna be easy.

40
00:02:00,055 --> 00:02:04,800
I have to explain everything as a way
to provide ammo to the troops.

41
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,844
And even though it's gonna be long,
I'm gonna say a lot.

42
00:02:07,844 --> 00:02:11,300
You know how some court cases drag on
weeks or months?

43
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:15,100
Well this is me putting my entire case
in this video.

44
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,571
So however long it is, it's me saying it all.

45
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:21,520
I'm going to explain what Games as a Service is,

46
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:24,424
why it's fraud, address any counterarguments,

47
00:02:24,424 --> 00:02:26,839
and most importantly, what can be done about it.

48
00:02:26,839 --> 00:02:28,339
I have a plan

49
00:02:28,339 --> 00:02:29,539
sort of.

50
00:02:29,539 --> 00:02:34,148
Oh and I think it's Games as a Service "is"
fraud and not "are" fraud

51
00:02:34,148 --> 00:02:38,096
because it's referring to the singular practice
"Games as a Service".

52
00:02:38,096 --> 00:02:43,630
But I guess you could also literally have
Games as a Service "are" something.

53
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:45,137
I really don't know if-

54
00:02:45,137 --> 00:02:49,280
So let's jump right in:
What is "Games as a Service?"

55
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,200
Well, Wikipedia defines it as

56
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,400
Ah ha ha! Gotcha!

57
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,180
No, we're not doing that.

58
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:56,700
They had their chance.

59
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:58,959
I was hoping they would make this easy on me.

60
00:02:58,959 --> 00:03:00,159
Nope.

61
00:03:00,159 --> 00:03:02,669
Gamasutra defines it as

62
00:03:02,669 --> 00:03:04,159
Uhh...

63
00:03:04,159 --> 00:03:07,220
This long winded philisophical ideal.

64
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:09,828
Ok, yeah, looks like I'm gonna have to
do this myself.

65
00:03:10,328 --> 00:03:13,110
Ok, first, let's get something out of the way.

66
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:15,084
Because this isn't confusing enough,

67
00:03:15,084 --> 00:03:19,800
"Games as a Service" is not the same as
a "game service".

68
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:22,785
A "game service" is usually a rental system.

69
00:03:22,785 --> 00:03:27,105
So if you pay a company twenty bucks a month
and can rent games or have them streamed to you,

70
00:03:27,105 --> 00:03:30,389
but you can also buy them outright somewhere,

71
00:03:30,389 --> 00:03:34,160
then that's not what I'm talking about.
That's not fraud, that's fine.

72
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:37,800
But "Games as a Service", how do we define that?

73
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:41,050
Well, it's a business practice, but what?

74
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:44,850
Well if you're a gamer, you probably have
an idea of what it is

75
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:49,650
so let's list some common traits and see if we
can narrow it down to what actually defines it.

76
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:52,239
Here's my list--let's go down the line.

77
00:03:52,239 --> 00:03:58,360
"Online only". Yes, I'd say that one defines
every use of Games as a Service.

78
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:02,119
Now some games have downloadable content
that you can run offline,

79
00:04:02,119 --> 00:04:05,477
but that's not what the industry means
when they refer to this.

80
00:04:05,477 --> 00:04:10,100
In fact, another term for Games as a
Service is "live service games".

81
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:13,660
In other words, you're connected online,
so it's live.

82
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:15,900
But hey, I'll give another freebie.

83
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:20,813
If offline Games as a Service games exist,
then those are not fraud.

84
00:04:20,813 --> 00:04:23,259
So hey, "Oblivion" horse armor

85
00:04:23,259 --> 00:04:24,559
Not fraud.

86
00:04:24,559 --> 00:04:28,447
This is actually a great baseline if you want
to determine how fair or not you think I'm

87
00:04:28,447 --> 00:04:33,377
being toward game companies. This says something
about where my standards are these days.

88
00:04:33,377 --> 00:04:38,808
But back to defining things, we'll say, "Yes,
every Games as a Service is online only."

89
00:04:38,808 --> 00:04:43,455
However, not every online game is Games
as a Service.

90
00:04:43,455 --> 00:04:45,378
We'll come back to that in a minute.

91
00:04:45,378 --> 00:04:47,683
"Extra online features"

92
00:04:47,683 --> 00:04:52,597
This is stuff like stat tracking,
matchmaking, player messaging, and so on.

93
00:04:52,597 --> 00:04:57,515
Well Games as a Service usually have
these, but they're by no means a guarantee.

94
00:04:57,515 --> 00:05:00,000
Plus, regular games can have these too.

95
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,359
Player messaging and voice chat go way back.

96
00:05:03,359 --> 00:05:06,667
Stat tracking certainly happened in
offline games.

97
00:05:06,667 --> 00:05:10,246
Matchmaking was usually third party software
in the past

98
00:05:10,246 --> 00:05:14,573
so I think it's safe to say none of those
define "Games as a Service".

99
00:05:14,573 --> 00:05:16,593
"Microtransactions".

100
00:05:16,593 --> 00:05:23,076
And you may not believe this, but NO,
those are NOT what define Games as a Service!

101
00:05:23,076 --> 00:05:28,225
Here are a few Games as a Service that
had NO microtransactions whatsoever.

102
00:05:28,225 --> 00:05:33,049
However, I will say the overwhelming
majority of Games as a Service

103
00:05:33,049 --> 00:05:35,080
DO have microtransactions

104
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:37,530
and this is the number one reason

105
00:05:37,530 --> 00:05:40,465
companies really like this practice.

106
00:05:40,465 --> 00:05:46,936
However, since exceptions do exist,
this is not actually what defines it.

107
00:05:46,936 --> 00:05:48,850
"Subscription fees".

108
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:52,130
Very few games still have these, so, no.

109
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:54,483
However, we have that trick again.

110
00:05:54,483 --> 00:05:58,260
Most Games as a Service do not have
a subscription fee,

111
00:05:58,260 --> 00:06:03,170
but all games with a subscription fee are
Games as a Service.

112
00:06:03,170 --> 00:06:04,170
Get it?

113
00:06:05,270 --> 00:06:07,289
"Frequent updates".

114
00:06:07,289 --> 00:06:10,550
Well a lot of good Games as a Service
have these, but again,

115
00:06:10,550 --> 00:06:12,750
it's by no means what defines them.

116
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:16,843
Plenty of Games as a Service go by years
with no updates at all

117
00:06:16,843 --> 00:06:18,787
so, nope, that's not it either.

118
00:06:19,287 --> 00:06:21,957
And, "massive multiplayer games".

119
00:06:21,957 --> 00:06:23,835
Nope, still not it.

120
00:06:23,835 --> 00:06:29,060
On one hand, you have some Games as a Service
that are intended for a small number of players,

121
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:33,770
on the other hand, you've had some gigantic
games that are not this practice.

122
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:36,560
The multiplayer mod for Just Cause 2

123
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:41,125
has had over 1,800 simultaneous players
in the game.

124
00:06:41,125 --> 00:06:44,841
Yet it is NOT considered "Games as a
Service".

125
00:06:44,841 --> 00:06:50,500
MMO games are usually Games as a Service,
but, not what defines it.

126
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,097
Okay, so wow.

127
00:06:52,097 --> 00:06:56,900
The only definition that survived is Games
as a Service are online only.

128
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:59,000
and even that one has a caveat.

129
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,006
See, some games are just meant to be played
online.

130
00:07:02,006 --> 00:07:04,600
If you play the game "Starsiege: Tribes"

131
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:07,863
the only way to really play it, is to
connect to other people.

132
00:07:07,863 --> 00:07:10,796
In essence, it's an online only game.

133
00:07:10,796 --> 00:07:15,226
Yet it is not Games as a Service.
You know why?

134
00:07:15,226 --> 00:07:18,383
Because YOU'RE still the one in control.

135
00:07:18,383 --> 00:07:22,990
Games as a Service means the company is
providing you the "service" of allowing

136
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:25,022
you to play your game.

137
00:07:25,022 --> 00:07:27,423
And yes, I am being facetious.

138
00:07:27,423 --> 00:07:30,370
You didn't used to need "permission" for that.

139
00:07:30,370 --> 00:07:33,385
Now people will debate what Games as a
Service should mean

140
00:07:33,385 --> 00:07:37,309
but this is the real world, hard definition.

141
00:07:37,309 --> 00:07:41,730
"Games as a Service" means the business
practice of players not having control

142
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:46,174
of whether they can play a game due to a
company withholding that function.

143
00:07:46,174 --> 00:07:50,838
That it all it is, in its purest,
most distilled form.

144
00:07:50,838 --> 00:07:53,810
Oh sure, they usually have other stuff on
this list

145
00:07:53,810 --> 00:07:57,354
but none of those other traits could survive
the definition test.

146
00:07:57,354 --> 00:08:02,350
In fact, I'd say this definition isn't even
the intent of Games as a Service.

147
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:04,810
But it is what defines it.

148
00:08:04,810 --> 00:08:09,246
If it doesn't have this, it is NOT Games
as a Service.

149
00:08:09,246 --> 00:08:11,250
Okay! Fraud time!

150
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:16,363
So now we have a concrete definition of
Games as a Service.

151
00:08:16,363 --> 00:08:18,330
But why is it fraud?

152
00:08:18,330 --> 00:08:22,785
Well, the simplest answer is it's being sold
as something it's not.

153
00:08:22,785 --> 00:08:24,307
I'll break it down.

154
00:08:24,307 --> 00:08:28,455
But first we have to understand the difference
between a "good" and a "service".

155
00:08:28,455 --> 00:08:31,934
Products are defined as "services" or
"goods".

156
00:08:31,934 --> 00:08:37,367
A "good" is something you buy and own,
a "service" is an action performed for you.

157
00:08:37,367 --> 00:08:40,420
So if you buy a hammer, it's a "good".

158
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:41,720
It's a thing.

159
00:08:41,770 --> 00:08:43,120
It's yours.

160
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,830
But if you get a haircut, that's a service.

161
00:08:47,830 --> 00:08:50,440
They didn't just hand you your haircut in a
box

162
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:53,270
they used scissors to deliver the desired
result.

163
00:08:53,270 --> 00:08:55,050
Or, so I'm told.

164
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:58,150
And again, I have to rail on Wikipedia here.

165
00:08:58,150 --> 00:09:03,640
Since they define goods as "something tangible"
then later say they're NOT always tangible.

166
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:07,500
Good job contradicting yourself in the first
sentence guys!

167
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,704
Whatever, that second part is correct.

168
00:09:09,704 --> 00:09:13,600
So things like digital movies you buy are
intangible goods.

169
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,540
You can't touch a movie.

170
00:09:15,540 --> 00:09:18,080
But it's still sold like a physical good.

171
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:22,281
Well guess what? Most games are goods too.

172
00:09:22,281 --> 00:09:24,980
Even Games as a Service.

173
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:30,331
So yes, I just said Games as a Service
are not services.

174
00:09:30,331 --> 00:09:31,531
I'll get to that.

175
00:09:31,531 --> 00:09:33,973
But, I have to concede on one point.

176
00:09:33,973 --> 00:09:38,968
If it's a game that has a subscription fee that
you pay each month like World of Warcraft

177
00:09:38,968 --> 00:09:42,205
under most laws, those ARE services

178
00:09:42,205 --> 00:09:44,822
and, I don't think I can make the case those
are fraud.

179
00:09:44,822 --> 00:09:46,755
Those win this round.

180
00:09:46,755 --> 00:09:50,983
But, you know what? We can work with that.
Since that's what, five games now?

181
00:09:51,383 --> 00:09:52,782
Eh, maybe ten.

182
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:58,948
But let's try to establish legally that
Games as a Service are not services.

183
00:09:58,948 --> 00:10:03,866
Now of course the law's different in every
country, but we'll cover some major ones.

184
00:10:03,866 --> 00:10:06,319
Now I can't take credit for most of this part.

185
00:10:06,319 --> 00:10:08,914
I found bits and crumbs of this on my own

186
00:10:08,914 --> 00:10:11,800
but then I stumbled into a mother lode of
information

187
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:15,063
on why games are goods and you own them.

188
00:10:15,063 --> 00:10:18,666
A highly researched user, Delicieuxz

189
00:10:18,666 --> 00:10:24,430
created the best layman's explanation on this
topic I've seen in the Linus Tech Tips forum.

190
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:27,930
Oh, hi Linus.

191
00:10:27,930 --> 00:10:33,300
Now I admit, this is a colorful title, but
putting that aside, he has some good logic.

192
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:35,300
So let's look at the abridged version.

193
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:40,966
"A license is a right to use a property or
intellectual property that belongs to somebody else."

194
00:10:40,966 --> 00:10:47,272
"When you read 'this software is licensed, not
sold' in a software end user license agreement"

195
00:10:47,272 --> 00:10:50,559
"'this software' refers to the software
intellectual property"

196
00:10:50,559 --> 00:10:56,567
"and not the copy of that intellectual property
that you've purchased via a software license."

197
00:10:56,567 --> 00:11:00,130
"The software intellectual property is
licensed, not sold."

198
00:11:00,130 --> 00:11:04,142
"The license is sold, not licensed or leased."

199
00:11:04,142 --> 00:11:06,486
A little confusing there, but let's keep going.

200
00:11:06,486 --> 00:11:09,901
"All the mass produced items you've bought
follow the same rules."

201
00:11:09,901 --> 00:11:14,039
"You are purchasing a one-off copy of the
intellectual property of those things"

202
00:11:14,039 --> 00:11:17,110
"and there is a transfer of ownership over
those instances"

203
00:11:17,110 --> 00:11:21,350
"and you become the sole owner of that
instance of intellectual property."

204
00:11:21,350 --> 00:11:24,719
"You own it and have full property rights
over it."

205
00:11:24,719 --> 00:11:29,269
Okay, just so everybody's with me, he's
saying if you buy a copy of...

206
00:11:29,269 --> 00:11:30,669
Half-Life

207
00:11:30,669 --> 00:11:34,030
you don't own the rights to the Half-Life
franchise.

208
00:11:34,030 --> 00:11:36,460
You can't sell additional copies of it.

209
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:40,385
But you DO own YOUR COPY of Half-Life.

210
00:11:40,385 --> 00:11:44,058
A lot of goods are like this. If you buy
a car

211
00:11:44,058 --> 00:11:47,995
you don't own the manufacturing and design
right to that car model.

212
00:11:47,995 --> 00:11:50,679
But you do own YOUR CAR.

213
00:11:50,679 --> 00:11:52,471
It's a basic concept.

214
00:11:52,471 --> 00:11:55,266
I said before I'm going up against a narrative

215
00:11:55,266 --> 00:12:00,076
well one myth you hear sometimes is you
don't "own" any of your software.

216
00:12:00,076 --> 00:12:04,160
What he's doing here is explaining why that's
almost always false.

217
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:06,414
Turns out you have ownership rights after all!

218
00:12:06,414 --> 00:12:07,914
Who knew?

219
00:12:07,914 --> 00:12:11,149
Next he says there are two types of software
licenses:

220
00:12:11,149 --> 00:12:14,046
perpetual, and subscription licenses.

221
00:12:14,046 --> 00:12:16,345
Perpetual ones last forever.

222
00:12:16,345 --> 00:12:21,471
So that copy of Mega Man that your brother
has in the attic? That's a perpetual license.

223
00:12:21,471 --> 00:12:25,634
And this is why World of Warcraft gets a
free pass for now.

224
00:12:25,634 --> 00:12:30,679
They want 15 bucks a month. That's as clear
as day a subscription license.

225
00:12:30,679 --> 00:12:33,820
If you're not paying a subscription fee for
your game

226
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:36,829
then it's being bought under a perpetual
license.

227
00:12:36,829 --> 00:12:40,700
That's a really key point, a whole lot of
things are gonna come back to this.

228
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:42,839
"A perpetual license is a product"

229
00:12:42,839 --> 00:12:48,178
"and whenever it's sold it undergoes transfer
of ownership upon the point of sale."

230
00:12:48,178 --> 00:12:52,860
"Ownership is what establishes one's decision
making authority over the thing."

231
00:12:52,860 --> 00:12:56,189
"To sell something is to relinquish it as
one's property"

232
00:12:56,189 --> 00:13:00,082
"and to relinquish all of one's decision
making authority over that thing"

233
00:13:00,082 --> 00:13:01,836
"to the person who bought it."

234
00:13:01,836 --> 00:13:06,444
"Then the seller no longer has any rightful
say over anything regarding that instance"

235
00:13:06,444 --> 00:13:09,759
"of software represented by its perpetual
license."

236
00:13:09,759 --> 00:13:10,959
So there it is.

237
00:13:10,959 --> 00:13:14,829
If you only have to pay once, and it's not
a subscription fee for your game

238
00:13:14,829 --> 00:13:19,616
then by definition it's being sold under
a perpetual license.

239
00:13:19,616 --> 00:13:25,230
Under most country's laws, that means it's
a GOOD, and NOT a service.

240
00:13:25,230 --> 00:13:30,500
This is why I wonder if "Games as a Service"
is a deliberate propaganda term.

241
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:34,900
It means the EXACT OPPOSITE OF WHAT IT SAYS!

242
00:13:34,900 --> 00:13:36,391
But I know what you're thinking.

243
00:13:36,391 --> 00:13:40,000
You're thinking "this is just some guy on
the internet saying words"

244
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,500
"that doesnt prove anything."

245
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:42,842
Fair point.

246
00:13:42,842 --> 00:13:45,076
But you know what helps a legal argument?

247
00:13:45,076 --> 00:13:49,034
If you back up what you're saying with legal
documents that say the same thing.

248
00:13:49,034 --> 00:13:52,393
And that's exactly what he does. For
multiple countries.

249
00:13:52,393 --> 00:13:57,038
"The European Union's highest court, the
Court of Justice, has ruled that software"

250
00:13:57,038 --> 00:14:01,710
"sold via a perpetual license represents a
good rather than a service"

251
00:14:01,710 --> 00:14:07,385
"and the purchaser becomes the exclusive owner
of that instance, just like a physical good."

252
00:14:07,385 --> 00:14:10,803
Then he links to the court documents for
UsedSoft v. Oracle

253
00:14:10,803 --> 00:14:13,722
and a more professional explanation of what
that means.

254
00:14:13,722 --> 00:14:16,961
Next up, Canada. He gives an outdated link

255
00:14:16,961 --> 00:14:21,858
but I found this court case which apparently
rules Software as a Service is a good

256
00:14:21,858 --> 00:14:23,839
As explained in this article.

257
00:14:23,839 --> 00:14:26,648
They even refer to Software as a Service!

258
00:14:26,648 --> 00:14:29,025
I love it when it's black and white like that.

259
00:14:29,025 --> 00:14:30,660
And later he revises things

260
00:14:30,660 --> 00:14:34,460
saying Canada follows the World Intellectual
Property Organization's

261
00:14:34,460 --> 00:14:38,223
International Classification of Goods and
Services Guidelines.

262
00:14:38,223 --> 00:14:40,959
Yeah you know we're having fun now with a
name like that.

263
00:14:40,959 --> 00:14:43,337
Anyway, they say they're goods too.

264
00:14:43,337 --> 00:14:46,797
Next, Australia. You may have heard of this
one.

265
00:14:46,797 --> 00:14:51,419
In the case of the Australian Competition
and Consumer Commission v. Valve Corporation

266
00:14:51,419 --> 00:14:55,172
Australia rules that sure enough, games
are goods.

267
00:14:55,172 --> 00:14:57,506
You have to love this quote in the conclusion

268
00:14:57,506 --> 00:15:02,700
"Each of Valve's challenges to the applicability
of the Australian Consumer Law fails."

269
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:08,472
Yada yada--"Valve supplied goods, which are
defined as including computer software."

270
00:15:08,872 --> 00:15:12,170
And then we get to the United States.

271
00:15:12,170 --> 00:15:16,049
Listen, the United States hard mode when it
comes to this topic.

272
00:15:16,049 --> 00:15:18,911
In the lower courts, nobody agrees on anything.

273
00:15:18,911 --> 00:15:22,550
In 2008 in the court case Vernor v. Autodesk

274
00:15:22,550 --> 00:15:27,090
a federal judge in Washington State said that
yes, you own the software.

275
00:15:27,090 --> 00:15:29,978
Then in 2010, that was appealed to the Ninth
Circuit

276
00:15:29,978 --> 00:15:35,649
which said no, you don't own the software and
you have to follow the EULA rules.

277
00:15:35,649 --> 00:15:40,489
But then there was an amicus brief filed with it
which said yeah, this could easily be abused

278
00:15:40,489 --> 00:15:44,907
could harm preservation, this is potentially
a dangerous precedent, and hey Congress

279
00:15:44,907 --> 00:15:46,906
why don't you do something about this?

280
00:15:46,906 --> 00:15:52,400
But even then, that decision was just from
the Ninth Circuit states, not the entire US

281
00:15:52,400 --> 00:15:54,090
Before I keep going, look at this!

282
00:15:54,090 --> 00:15:58,503
That giant headline saying you don't own
anything. That's misleading.

283
00:15:58,503 --> 00:16:01,181
This is what I mean when I talk about a
narrative.

284
00:16:01,181 --> 00:16:05,329
Especially because in 2013 the Supreme Court
ruled on a different case

285
00:16:05,329 --> 00:16:08,719
saying people in the U.S can resell
copyrighted goods

286
00:16:08,719 --> 00:16:13,547
which includes software licenses, and the
first sale doctrine applies

287
00:16:13,547 --> 00:16:17,704
which states a seller retains no decision
making authority over a good

288
00:16:17,704 --> 00:16:20,057
once they have sold it to someone else.

289
00:16:20,057 --> 00:16:23,760
And as a side note, the official link to this
court document was down.

290
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:26,063
I had to find it using the Wayback Machine

291
00:16:26,063 --> 00:16:30,146
so I guess that doesn't bode too well for the
Supreme Court, but, whatever.

292
00:16:30,146 --> 00:16:34,416
In the US, games as services being goods
aren't quite proven

293
00:16:34,416 --> 00:16:37,443
but there's a lot of precedent that implies
they are.

294
00:16:38,043 --> 00:16:39,768
Delicieuxz says it best:

295
00:16:39,768 --> 00:16:44,318
"A specific matter of software ownership has
never gone to the USA's Supreme Court"

296
00:16:44,318 --> 00:16:47,669
"and it's likely that software publishers
would prefer that it doesn't."

297
00:16:47,669 --> 00:16:49,069
He's damn right they do!

298
00:16:49,069 --> 00:16:53,456
"Because in all likeliness the verdict will
be the same as it was in the European Union"

299
00:16:53,456 --> 00:16:57,956
"and in Australia. Because this matter has
never gone to the USA's Supreme Court"

300
00:16:57,956 --> 00:17:02,245
"and because regional court verdicts have
conflicted with each other in their conclusions"

301
00:17:02,245 --> 00:17:07,685
"it is baseless for anyone to claim that people
in the USA don't own their purchased software."

302
00:17:08,085 --> 00:17:12,092
So again, if there's no subscription fee,
you own your games.

303
00:17:12,092 --> 00:17:13,476
Well so what?

304
00:17:13,476 --> 00:17:18,554
Just because Games as a Service isn't actually
a service, that doesn't mean anything.

305
00:17:18,554 --> 00:17:21,104
It's just a name for a business practice.

306
00:17:21,104 --> 00:17:23,061
Where does the fraud come in?

307
00:17:23,061 --> 00:17:26,383
Well the "fraud" comes from that whole
ownership thing.

308
00:17:26,383 --> 00:17:31,183
Remember: a seller retains NO decision making
authority over a product

309
00:17:31,183 --> 00:17:33,639
once they have sold it to someone else.

310
00:17:33,639 --> 00:17:38,487
Now some of you may know that I'm a
huge advocate against killing games.

311
00:17:38,487 --> 00:17:43,483
By "killing games," I mean the practice of
a company's actions leaving a game completely

312
00:17:43,483 --> 00:17:46,454
unplayable by anyone who bought it.

313
00:17:46,454 --> 00:17:49,141
This is also known as "bricking" a game.

314
00:17:49,141 --> 00:17:54,121
Well killing games, and Games as a Service
are handcuffed together

315
00:17:54,121 --> 00:17:56,518
You almost don't have one without the other.

316
00:17:56,918 --> 00:18:01,909
See, all Games as a Service depend on you
connecting to a server controlled by a company.

317
00:18:01,909 --> 00:18:03,841
That's fine while the game is running,

318
00:18:03,841 --> 00:18:07,910
but eventually most companies decide they're
not making enough money on the game anymore

319
00:18:07,910 --> 00:18:09,784
to justify the server running.

320
00:18:09,784 --> 00:18:11,400
So they shut it down.

321
00:18:11,400 --> 00:18:16,832
Once THAT happens, every single person who
bought the game can never play it again.

322
00:18:16,832 --> 00:18:19,314
If I sold you a copy of a game on disc

323
00:18:19,314 --> 00:18:23,437
then next month while you were sleeping I
snuck into your house and broke the disc

324
00:18:23,437 --> 00:18:25,037
I would go to jail.

325
00:18:25,037 --> 00:18:30,442
In practical terms, that's almost exactly
what Games as a Service is.

326
00:18:30,442 --> 00:18:35,321
Companies engaged in this practice almost
always destroy your product

327
00:18:35,321 --> 00:18:37,064
AFTER they've sold it to you.

328
00:18:37,464 --> 00:18:41,498
And I keep saying this "almost always"
happens, there's a reason for that.

329
00:18:41,898 --> 00:18:45,450
Some volunteers and I have put together a
list of every game we could find

330
00:18:45,450 --> 00:18:48,067
that has been shut down, and how that was
handled.

331
00:18:48,067 --> 00:18:50,278
Now if anything this is the small list

332
00:18:50,278 --> 00:18:55,010
since I filtered out games like you see here just
to bring focus on the bigger problem cases.

333
00:18:55,010 --> 00:18:59,726
We came up with 122 titles that mostly meet
that criteria.

334
00:18:59,726 --> 00:19:05,144
Now this list may not be 100% accurate. It
can be difficult to verify some information

335
00:19:05,144 --> 00:19:08,382
but I'm guessing it's at least 95% accurate.

336
00:19:08,382 --> 00:19:12,574
And it's probably the most extensive data
collected on this topic to date.

337
00:19:12,574 --> 00:19:18,174
So, out of 122 titles, how many times did
the companies do something to either

338
00:19:18,174 --> 00:19:22,677
A) Give customers a chance to run their game
after shutdown, or

339
00:19:22,677 --> 00:19:26,570
B) Give a full refund, no strings attached.

340
00:19:26,570 --> 00:19:29,178
Well from my best count, here it is.

341
00:19:29,578 --> 00:19:32,057
Yeah. That's five titles.

342
00:19:32,057 --> 00:19:36,483
Or in other words, about 4% of all
Games as a Service.

343
00:19:36,483 --> 00:19:39,644
So hey, I made a clickbait-y title, I'll
fess up.

344
00:19:39,644 --> 00:19:45,567
If we're being completely honest, "Games as
a Service" is not fraud.

345
00:19:45,567 --> 00:19:49,417
It's only fraud about 96% of the time.

346
00:19:49,417 --> 00:19:54,013
So if anyone says I'm exaggerating how bad
all this is, it's true.

347
00:19:54,013 --> 00:19:55,795
I'm exaggerating 4%.

348
00:19:56,745 --> 00:19:58,744
And who knows? We could have missed one!

349
00:19:58,744 --> 00:20:02,201
I'm making all this public, you're welcome
to double check the data.

350
00:20:02,201 --> 00:20:05,960
In fact, I'm not sure which of those games
were on a subscription license

351
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:10,586
at the time of shutdown, so you might even
be able to get that number down to 95

352
00:20:10,586 --> 00:20:12,750
hey, maybe 94%.

353
00:20:13,150 --> 00:20:16,600
Oh, and some of these titles you can
actually still run

354
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:22,000
usually due to illicit server emulators. But
those are cases where the companies did NOTHING

355
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:27,384
to help, and it was in spite of the company's
actions. Not because of them.

356
00:20:27,384 --> 00:20:28,610
More on that in a bit.

357
00:20:29,010 --> 00:20:33,170
So I'm calling this fraud because of the
reality, and the intent.

358
00:20:33,170 --> 00:20:36,539
Just because your game doesn't run
doesn't make it fraud.

359
00:20:36,539 --> 00:20:39,730
There could be bugs, the hardware could go
bad.

360
00:20:39,730 --> 00:20:43,813
I'm not talking about quirks, accidents,
forces of nature.

361
00:20:43,813 --> 00:20:47,945
But with Games as a Service, the product
is DESIGNED to fail

362
00:20:47,945 --> 00:20:50,334
as soon as they shut down the server.

363
00:20:50,334 --> 00:20:55,891
The fraud begins once they purposefully
take away access to your product.

364
00:20:55,891 --> 00:21:00,958
Games as a Service is fraud because it
involves selling perpetual licenses

365
00:21:00,958 --> 00:21:04,293
that are in practice, NOT perpetual.

366
00:21:04,293 --> 00:21:09,617
The decision making authority over the product
is being removed from the buyer.

367
00:21:09,617 --> 00:21:13,528
Customers buy games with the expectation
that they will function.

368
00:21:13,528 --> 00:21:20,420
If 100% of all copies sold of a game cease to
function because of deliberate interference

369
00:21:20,420 --> 00:21:26,125
from the seller AFTER the point of sale, how
can that be considered an honest practice?

370
00:21:26,625 --> 00:21:31,809
Now the opposition would say this isn't fraud,
because you were sold a perpetual license

371
00:21:31,809 --> 00:21:37,023
and just because the company breaks your
game, it doesn't mean you lost your license.

372
00:21:37,023 --> 00:21:41,116
You are still free to "repair" the game,
after they break it.

373
00:21:41,566 --> 00:21:45,451
I say this is a disingenuous argument on
multiple levels.

374
00:21:45,451 --> 00:21:49,462
If somebody sells you a bike, and then
later you get a flat tire

375
00:21:49,462 --> 00:21:53,497
yes, your product no longer works, and it
takes some effort to repair it.

376
00:21:53,497 --> 00:21:57,625
But that's something the average person can
be reasonably expected to do.

377
00:21:57,625 --> 00:21:59,781
Or hey, take it to a repair shop!

378
00:21:59,781 --> 00:22:01,000
More importantly

379
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:06,030
the company that sold you the bike didn't
come to your house and puncture the tire.

380
00:22:06,030 --> 00:22:08,438
I see that as an important distinction.

381
00:22:08,438 --> 00:22:11,050
Now when a Game as a Service stops working

382
00:22:11,050 --> 00:22:14,796
which again, I want to emphasize is a
deliberate act

383
00:22:14,796 --> 00:22:19,672
NO ONE on EARTH could be "reasonably
expected" to fix that.

384
00:22:19,672 --> 00:22:21,900
The reason for that is two-fold.

385
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:25,900
First, it takes specialized programming
knowledge in order to recreate

386
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:28,110
the portions of the server that were lost.

387
00:22:28,110 --> 00:22:32,918
Again, the way these games work is most of
the data is on the customer's computer.

388
00:22:32,918 --> 00:22:36,780
But the company hosting the game has key
information about how to run it.

389
00:22:36,780 --> 00:22:39,767
Where things spawn, logic routines, and
so on.

390
00:22:39,767 --> 00:22:43,624
If you want, you can almost think of this
as the "body" and this as the "brain".

391
00:22:43,624 --> 00:22:45,361
When they shut the "brain" down here

392
00:22:45,361 --> 00:22:48,970
a large portion of the game is now missing,
and it's unplayable.

393
00:22:48,970 --> 00:22:53,123
In order to run the game again, the person
would have to write a new "brain".

394
00:22:53,123 --> 00:22:54,423
I mean--server!

395
00:22:54,423 --> 00:22:59,379
Now that is an exhorbitant amount of work,
and takes a lot of specialized knowledge.

396
00:22:59,379 --> 00:23:04,285
I would estimate only 5% of the population
is even capable of doing that.

397
00:23:04,285 --> 00:23:06,522
However, it is possible.

398
00:23:06,522 --> 00:23:11,500
Whether that's a reasonable expectation of
buyers to repair their product is debatable.

399
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:15,778
I think it isn't, but that is NOTHING
compared to the next part.

400
00:23:15,778 --> 00:23:18,767
Programming a new server is difficult enough.

401
00:23:18,767 --> 00:23:23,950
But companies will also encrypt their data in
order to protect against hackers and piracy.

402
00:23:23,950 --> 00:23:27,640
This is known as "Digital Rights Management,"
or DRM.

403
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:33,057
Now that's reasonable while the product is
still being sold, but once it's shut down

404
00:23:33,057 --> 00:23:37,349
then this is the equivalent of locking things
up and throwing away the key.

405
00:23:37,349 --> 00:23:41,126
I've talked to a developer for a resurrected
game server emulator

406
00:23:41,126 --> 00:23:45,493
and he said they had a cryptography
expert helping them decrypt the code

407
00:23:45,493 --> 00:23:47,781
and even then, it took them years.

408
00:23:47,781 --> 00:23:51,796
So if you were qualified to crack codes
like what the Nazis were using

409
00:23:51,796 --> 00:23:58,548
and win World War II in Europe, then yes, you
might be qualified to "repair" your game.

410
00:23:58,548 --> 00:24:01,082
I'm really not exaggerating.

411
00:24:01,082 --> 00:24:06,012
Not only is that such a small percentage of
the population capable of doing that

412
00:24:06,012 --> 00:24:10,360
but even for them, that in no way can be
considered reasonable.

413
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:14,813
For most people, I'm talking 99.9% or more

414
00:24:14,813 --> 00:24:18,896
it is IMPOSSIBLE for them to repair their
game after shutdown.

415
00:24:19,396 --> 00:24:23,511
So, not literally impossible, but, again,
I'm rounding.

416
00:24:24,211 --> 00:24:26,908
But let's try to shore this up a little
legally.

417
00:24:26,908 --> 00:24:30,140
And again, the United States is hard mode.

418
00:24:30,140 --> 00:24:33,691
Some of you may have heard of the term
"planned obsolescence".

419
00:24:33,691 --> 00:24:37,833
That's the practice of designing goods to
intentionally break faster

420
00:24:37,833 --> 00:24:41,145
in hopes that you'll buy a new one so they
can make more money.

421
00:24:41,145 --> 00:24:45,558
Best I can tell, this is perfectly legal in
the United States unfortunately.

422
00:24:45,558 --> 00:24:49,539
But there's a subset of that called
"programmed obsolescence".

423
00:24:49,539 --> 00:24:54,202
See unlike planned obsolescence, which just
increases the odds the product will break

424
00:24:54,202 --> 00:24:58,762
over time, programmed obsolescence
ENSURES it will break

425
00:24:58,768 --> 00:25:01,995
on purpose, usually at a specific time.

426
00:25:01,995 --> 00:25:06,248
And it's not clear if THAT is legal in the
US or not.

427
00:25:06,248 --> 00:25:13,070
There have been some cases on it. Like class
action lawsuit Jackie Blennis v. HP in 2008.

428
00:25:13,070 --> 00:25:18,480
Charges were pressed against Hewlett-Packard for
designing ink cartridges to shut down prematurely.

429
00:25:18,480 --> 00:25:25,284
HP denied any wrongdoing, and yet awarded the
complaintants $5 million of credit to shut them up.

430
00:25:25,284 --> 00:25:27,893
Yeah, I'm sure they did that just to be nice.

431
00:25:27,893 --> 00:25:31,411
Otherwise, why wouldn't they fight it and
maintain their innocence?

432
00:25:31,411 --> 00:25:37,550
Especially since they got hit with another
class action lawsuit, San Miguel v. HP in 2016

433
00:25:37,550 --> 00:25:42,398
for updating firmware on printers to stop
accepting third party cartridges.

434
00:25:42,398 --> 00:25:44,945
In other words, breaking functionality.

435
00:25:44,945 --> 00:25:49,800
THIS time they were guilty of wrongdoing
and had to pay out $1.5 million.

436
00:25:49,800 --> 00:25:54,626
Now these both imply that programmed
obsolescence could be illegal if challenged

437
00:25:54,626 --> 00:25:59,610
however in both cases the reasoning behind
the charges doesn't tie directly to that.

438
00:25:59,610 --> 00:26:03,096
So these aren't the strongest legal
precendents in my opinion.

439
00:26:03,096 --> 00:26:09,280
The strongest legal argument I DID find was
in 2017 in Impression v. Lexmark.

440
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:12,382
The Supreme Court ruled that you have
ownership of your product

441
00:26:12,382 --> 00:26:14,951
and are free to do with it as you please

442
00:26:14,951 --> 00:26:18,542
even if patents still apply to it that
control its behavior.

443
00:26:18,542 --> 00:26:22,712
While that's still a bit of a stretch to say
programmed obsolescence isn't legal

444
00:26:22,712 --> 00:26:25,421
I thought these lines from the ruling were noteworthy.

445
00:26:25,421 --> 00:26:28,633
"Even if the restrictions in Lexmark's
contracts with its customers"

446
00:26:28,633 --> 00:26:31,619
"were clear and enforceable under contract law"

447
00:26:31,619 --> 00:26:37,385
"they do not entitle Lexmark to retain patent
rights in an item that it has elected to sell."

448
00:26:37,421 --> 00:26:42,669
"Once a patentee sells an item, it has
secured that reward, and the patent laws"

449
00:26:42,669 --> 00:26:47,088
"provide no basis for restraining the use and
enjoyment of the product."

450
00:26:47,088 --> 00:26:49,387
That might be a key sentence.

451
00:26:49,387 --> 00:26:53,596
I would call shutting down a game so that
the person who bought it has no way to ever

452
00:26:53,596 --> 00:26:58,772
run it again "restraining the use and
enjoyment of the product," wouldn't you?

453
00:26:58,772 --> 00:27:00,653
There might be something there.

454
00:27:00,653 --> 00:27:05,795
But, this hasn't been tested in court, so
most of this is in a legal gray zone.

455
00:27:05,795 --> 00:27:09,953
That means the law hasn't specifically said
if something is legal or not

456
00:27:09,953 --> 00:27:12,587
so everybody has to guess based on other
laws.

457
00:27:12,587 --> 00:27:16,473
I'm obviously on the "this is fraud" side

458
00:27:16,473 --> 00:27:21,086
because if the law says one thing, and the
outcome is the exact opposite

459
00:27:21,086 --> 00:27:23,014
then something's breaking down.

460
00:27:23,014 --> 00:27:26,484
But how does this actually play out?
Let's see.

461
00:27:26,484 --> 00:27:28,513
Do you own your game?

462
00:27:28,513 --> 00:27:30,975
Theoretically, yes.

463
00:27:30,975 --> 00:27:35,579
You technically never lose ownership of your
perpetual license. It's yours.

464
00:27:35,579 --> 00:27:38,094
In practice? Unclear.

465
00:27:38,094 --> 00:27:42,127
There's been no higher court ruling on
whether Games as a Service infringes

466
00:27:42,127 --> 00:27:43,826
upon your perpetual license.

467
00:27:43,826 --> 00:27:46,025
THIS is what I'm trying to change.

468
00:27:46,025 --> 00:27:50,890
In real world terms, also known as the
only part I actually care about

469
00:27:50,890 --> 00:27:55,471
NO. If no normal person can ever be expected
to use their product again

470
00:27:55,471 --> 00:28:00,571
after the company disables it, that is
certainly effective loss of ownership.

471
00:28:00,571 --> 00:28:02,004
Seller intent?

472
00:28:02,004 --> 00:28:06,205
NO. Games as a Service are designed to
have their functionality removed

473
00:28:06,205 --> 00:28:08,239
at a later, undisclosed date

474
00:28:08,239 --> 00:28:12,471
AND the data is encrypted to prevent you
from restoring it yourself.

475
00:28:12,471 --> 00:28:16,095
It doesn't get any more deliberate to remove
your ownership than that.

476
00:28:16,095 --> 00:28:21,300
Games as a Service is a situation where the
outcome is the exact opposite

477
00:28:21,300 --> 00:28:23,542
of the intent of the law.

478
00:28:23,542 --> 00:28:27,733
This is why the myth that why you DON'T own
your games keeps persisting!

479
00:28:27,733 --> 00:28:30,729
People are just saying what they're seeing
with their own eyes!

480
00:28:30,729 --> 00:28:34,465
Laws are MEANINGLESS if they're not enforced!

481
00:28:34,465 --> 00:28:37,601
And, that's all I have for U.S law.

482
00:28:37,601 --> 00:28:41,671
But fortunately, things are a little more
optimistic in other countries.

483
00:28:41,671 --> 00:28:45,488
In Germany, there's a political party that
initiated a study to try and reduce

484
00:28:45,488 --> 00:28:48,878
planned obsolescence using existing laws
on the books.

485
00:28:48,878 --> 00:28:50,703
So kind of like what I'm doing.

486
00:28:50,703 --> 00:28:52,766
Some parts I found noteworthy.

487
00:28:52,766 --> 00:28:55,589
"Introduction of minimum standards for
repair."

488
00:28:55,589 --> 00:28:59,283
"Obligations to provide repair instructions."

489
00:28:59,283 --> 00:29:02,452
In France, the Hamon Law was introducted in
2014

490
00:29:02,452 --> 00:29:05,358
to more or less make planned obsolescence
illegal.

491
00:29:05,358 --> 00:29:08,506
Except that it looks like that it only
applies to physical goods

492
00:29:08,506 --> 00:29:10,828
so there may or may not be an opening there.

493
00:29:10,828 --> 00:29:15,241
And hey, it's being put to the test suing
printer companies Epson, HP, Canon

494
00:29:15,241 --> 00:29:17,761
and Brother for violating this law.

495
00:29:17,761 --> 00:29:21,295
Would be great if they could establish
a precedent for software too.

496
00:29:21,295 --> 00:29:24,750
Boy, printers are the games industry of the
real world, huh?

497
00:29:25,350 --> 00:29:26,696
And in Europe in general

498
00:29:26,696 --> 00:29:30,336
the European Parliament accepted a resolution
to lengthen consumer goods'

499
00:29:30,336 --> 00:29:34,437
and software's longevity to combat planned
obsolescence.

500
00:29:34,437 --> 00:29:36,057
And back to Australia

501
00:29:36,057 --> 00:29:40,298
the Australian Competition and Consumer
Commission has consumer guarantees

502
00:29:40,298 --> 00:29:45,493
including that your product is safe, LASTING,
and with no faults.

503
00:29:45,493 --> 00:29:50,369
Well, Games as a Service are safe, so, you
know, one out of three.

504
00:29:50,369 --> 00:29:54,893
Now it also says this doesn't apply if you
knew of, or were made aware of the faults

505
00:29:54,893 --> 00:29:56,593
before you bought the product.

506
00:29:56,593 --> 00:29:58,735
This is another gray area again.

507
00:29:58,735 --> 00:30:03,532
The company usually DOES tell you Games as
a Service could cease working at any time

508
00:30:03,532 --> 00:30:05,765
in the end user license agreement.

509
00:30:05,765 --> 00:30:08,950
But is this really following the intent of
the law?

510
00:30:08,950 --> 00:30:12,444
If the company is creating the defect in the
product on purpose

511
00:30:12,444 --> 00:30:15,438
and does it OVER and OVER again

512
00:30:15,438 --> 00:30:18,275
to the point where selling intentionally
defective products

513
00:30:18,275 --> 00:30:20,254
is their whole business model

514
00:30:20,254 --> 00:30:24,444
that still smells like fraud to me, and
should at least be examined.

515
00:30:24,444 --> 00:30:27,251
Now I don't know the details of these laws
or movements

516
00:30:27,251 --> 00:30:31,760
since it's hard enough for me to research
this as it is, let alone in different languages

517
00:30:31,760 --> 00:30:34,900
and it looks like they're mostly focused
on physical goods

518
00:30:34,900 --> 00:30:39,458
but for God's sake, we've got to get software
on board this train.

519
00:30:39,458 --> 00:30:43,677
Just requiring companies to provide "repair
instructions"

520
00:30:43,677 --> 00:30:47,460
would be NIGHT and DAY compared to where
we are now.

521
00:30:47,460 --> 00:30:50,973
The bottom line is, it looks like a lot of
people are getting upset

522
00:30:50,973 --> 00:30:53,666
over companies ripping customers off

523
00:30:53,666 --> 00:30:57,576
and the law is playing catch-up with what
technology is doing.

524
00:30:57,576 --> 00:31:00,651
Games as a Service are goods in these
countries

525
00:31:00,651 --> 00:31:04,184
which means there's a real chance they could
be protected under the law.

526
00:31:04,184 --> 00:31:09,619
The INTENT of these laws is almost identical
to my war on Games as a Service.

527
00:31:09,619 --> 00:31:15,484
Software has got to piggyback onto these laws
and get examined by the right governing bodies.

528
00:31:15,984 --> 00:31:19,672
And THAT is IT for LEGAL ARGUMENTS!

529
00:31:19,672 --> 00:31:21,290
AHHHHHH!

530
00:31:21,990 --> 00:31:23,371
I'm taking a break.

531
00:31:23,371 --> 00:31:25,721
You want to take a break? I'm taking a break.

532
00:31:28,649 --> 00:31:39,988
[8-bit music playing]

533
00:31:42,911 --> 00:31:45,937
Okay, so those were the legal arguments.

534
00:31:45,937 --> 00:31:49,033
Yeah, that was...fun.

535
00:31:51,602 --> 00:31:56,632
But I need to cover EVERYTHING, so,
let's get conceptual!

536
00:31:56,632 --> 00:32:00,762
Maybe you're thinking, "Okay, so what if the
law says games are goods?"

537
00:32:00,762 --> 00:32:04,651
"Maybe they're really services and the law's
just outdated?"

538
00:32:04,651 --> 00:32:08,887
Okay. Let's pretend the law doesn't exist.
We're writing it fresh.

539
00:32:08,887 --> 00:32:11,718
If that was the case, then Games as a
Service

540
00:32:11,718 --> 00:32:15,605
would be unlike any other service that
exists.

541
00:32:15,605 --> 00:32:17,105
I'm not joking.

542
00:32:17,151 --> 00:32:22,881
Every other service I can think of, has at
least ONE of the following conditions.

543
00:32:22,881 --> 00:32:25,961
One, an expectation of how long it lasts.

544
00:32:25,961 --> 00:32:29,347
If you pay an electric bill, it's usually
for a month at a time.

545
00:32:29,347 --> 00:32:33,965
Or if you go to an amusement park. Your
ticket lasts the day, or the season.

546
00:32:33,965 --> 00:32:37,625
The point being, everybody knows when the
service is over and it ends

547
00:32:37,625 --> 00:32:39,593
or you have to renew it.

548
00:32:39,593 --> 00:32:43,157
Two, there's a general expectation of when
it's done.

549
00:32:43,157 --> 00:32:45,304
If you pay roofers to fix your roof

550
00:32:45,304 --> 00:32:49,461
you may have no idea how long that will take.
But you know when it's fixed.

551
00:32:49,461 --> 00:32:52,712
There's a rough idea of when the service is finished.

552
00:32:52,712 --> 00:32:57,534
Three, there are real-world limitations
towards keeping the service going.

553
00:32:57,534 --> 00:32:59,725
Some services can go on forever.

554
00:32:59,725 --> 00:33:02,149
If I rent a plot of land

555
00:33:02,149 --> 00:33:05,807
I don't have to do anything but collect my
money each month pretty much.

556
00:33:05,807 --> 00:33:07,420
Hence number one here.

557
00:33:07,420 --> 00:33:09,231
In fact I've had a landlord before

558
00:33:09,231 --> 00:33:12,263
where at one point, I wasn't sure if he
was alive or dead.

559
00:33:12,263 --> 00:33:14,549
But, the service kept going!

560
00:33:14,549 --> 00:33:18,599
But most services require real resources
to continue.

561
00:33:18,599 --> 00:33:22,541
If you go to a concert, and you're having
a good time, and just want to stay

562
00:33:22,541 --> 00:33:25,864
Well that's great, but the band has to stop
sometime.

563
00:33:25,864 --> 00:33:28,268
Eventually, they're going to run out of coke.

564
00:33:28,268 --> 00:33:31,661
Maybe you can stump me, but I couldn't think
of ONE service

565
00:33:31,661 --> 00:33:35,021
that didn't require at least ONE of those
conditions.

566
00:33:35,021 --> 00:33:38,225
EXCEPT, Games as a Service.

567
00:33:38,225 --> 00:33:43,388
Hence, even on a conceptual level, they're
NOT SERVICES.

568
00:33:43,388 --> 00:33:44,749
Here, I'll show you.

569
00:33:44,749 --> 00:33:47,489
"Expectation of how long it lasts."

570
00:33:47,489 --> 00:33:50,538
Well, subscription games like World of
Warcraft

571
00:33:50,538 --> 00:33:52,903
yes, they win this round too.

572
00:33:52,903 --> 00:33:54,230
Dammit!

573
00:33:54,230 --> 00:33:58,422
Subscription games really are services, and
they're not fraud.

574
00:33:58,422 --> 00:34:00,467
I'll have to get them on the final round.

575
00:34:01,184 --> 00:34:05,444
So besides those few games that still require
a monthly fee to play

576
00:34:05,444 --> 00:34:07,659
no, this doesn't apply.

577
00:34:07,659 --> 00:34:11,846
If there's an expectation for how long
Games as a Service last

578
00:34:11,846 --> 00:34:13,211
then what is it?

579
00:34:13,211 --> 00:34:16,490
The Culling 2 lasted a week and a half.

580
00:34:16,490 --> 00:34:20,987
Meridian 59 has been going on over 22 years.

581
00:34:20,987 --> 00:34:22,370
That's no help!

582
00:34:22,370 --> 00:34:24,276
Well what do the games themselves tell you?

583
00:34:24,276 --> 00:34:26,275
Well most don't tell you anything.

584
00:34:26,275 --> 00:34:29,791
They just say they can be shut down at any
time, for any reason.

585
00:34:29,791 --> 00:34:33,764
Some say they can be shut down at any time,
plus 30 days.

586
00:34:33,764 --> 00:34:36,088
Even then, I'd argue nobody buying the game

587
00:34:36,088 --> 00:34:41,060
is actually expecting it to stop working
after 30 days.

588
00:34:41,060 --> 00:34:44,102
Now I'd say there's no standard here, but
that's not true.

589
00:34:44,102 --> 00:34:46,234
There IS a standard for games.

590
00:34:46,234 --> 00:34:48,008
The traditional standard is

591
00:34:48,008 --> 00:34:49,193
forever!

592
00:34:49,193 --> 00:34:52,221
There are people who speedrun Super Mario
Brothers.

593
00:34:52,221 --> 00:34:54,532
That game's over 30 years old.

594
00:34:54,532 --> 00:34:57,310
So, yeah, "forever," that's a good standard.

595
00:34:57,310 --> 00:35:00,757
Games as a Service is trying to push a
new standard.

596
00:35:00,757 --> 00:35:02,162
Except it doesn't have one!

597
00:35:02,162 --> 00:35:04,394
Just that it's less, for you!

598
00:35:04,394 --> 00:35:08,518
Oh, except for The Secret World, and,
Fallout 76.

599
00:35:08,518 --> 00:35:11,128
They're both saying you'll be able to play
them forever.

600
00:35:11,848 --> 00:35:12,643
Umm...

601
00:35:12,943 --> 00:35:13,999
Okay?

602
00:35:14,443 --> 00:35:19,009
Well, for every OTHER Game as a Service
that DOESN'T last forever

603
00:35:19,009 --> 00:35:23,530
this is a concrete example of how your
rights are being taken away from you.

604
00:35:23,530 --> 00:35:27,793
We've gone from "play forever," to
"whatever the seller feels like".

605
00:35:27,793 --> 00:35:29,920
So, no, this does not apply.

606
00:35:30,532 --> 00:35:33,278
Expectation of when it's done.

607
00:35:33,278 --> 00:35:36,798
Yeah, let me ask you, when is somebody
"done" with a game?

608
00:35:36,798 --> 00:35:38,213
When they beat it?

609
00:35:38,213 --> 00:35:40,089
What if they want to play it again?

610
00:35:40,089 --> 00:35:44,040
What if it's a game that has no ending,
it's just sort of an ongoing thing?

611
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:47,483
What if they want to come back and play it
after a few years?

612
00:35:47,483 --> 00:35:50,141
Exactly, there's no standard at all here.

613
00:35:50,141 --> 00:35:52,795
Well, no, that's not right either.

614
00:35:52,795 --> 00:35:54,877
There IS a standard for games.

615
00:35:54,877 --> 00:35:57,927
The old standard used to be that YOU own
the game

616
00:35:57,927 --> 00:36:00,597
so, YOU decide when you're done with it.

617
00:36:00,597 --> 00:36:02,788
And that's gonna be different for every
person.

618
00:36:02,788 --> 00:36:05,705
So no "general" standard.

619
00:36:05,705 --> 00:36:06,805
GNAHH!

620
00:36:06,805 --> 00:36:08,955
Real world limitations.

621
00:36:08,955 --> 00:36:10,983
Okay let me explain this one.

622
00:36:10,983 --> 00:36:15,463
Remember that body and brain analogy I made
to the server and clients earlier?

623
00:36:15,463 --> 00:36:18,468
Well the vast majority of videogames
throughout history

624
00:36:18,468 --> 00:36:21,268
have been sold with both the body, AND
the brain.

625
00:36:21,268 --> 00:36:25,952
You got the whole package, so you could keep
the game running as long as you wanted.

626
00:36:25,952 --> 00:36:27,880
Now with the server and client model

627
00:36:27,880 --> 00:36:31,197
they're set up so that the brain here needs
maintenance by real people

628
00:36:31,197 --> 00:36:34,629
and computer hardware, and electricity
bills, and so on.

629
00:36:34,629 --> 00:36:37,411
But that's a CHOICE, not a NECESSITY.

630
00:36:37,411 --> 00:36:40,490
See at their core, videogames are CODE.

631
00:36:40,490 --> 00:36:41,890
They're NUMBERS.

632
00:36:41,890 --> 00:36:44,240
They don't "wear out" over time.

633
00:36:44,240 --> 00:36:49,138
The number "three" works just as well now as
it did thousands of years ago.

634
00:36:49,138 --> 00:36:50,862
It just looks a little different.

635
00:36:50,862 --> 00:36:53,997
The containers that hold the data can wear
out

636
00:36:53,997 --> 00:36:57,235
but it can also be copied from one container
to the next.

637
00:36:57,235 --> 00:36:59,228
So there's an infinite supply!

638
00:36:59,228 --> 00:37:02,060
Don't worry! We're not gonna run outta
numbers!

639
00:37:02,060 --> 00:37:03,867
They're always on tap!

640
00:37:03,867 --> 00:37:08,454
Now the server is usually maintained LIKE a
service while it's running

641
00:37:08,454 --> 00:37:12,425
but once it's shut down, it COULD just be
copied over here

642
00:37:12,425 --> 00:37:14,760
and someone ELSE could get it running.

643
00:37:14,760 --> 00:37:19,332
Companies almost never DO that, but the point
is the limitation isn't real.

644
00:37:19,332 --> 00:37:24,906
It's artificial. Somebody made it up. They
could just as easily undo it.

645
00:37:24,906 --> 00:37:30,098
In fact, it's easier to undo this, rather
than set this split up in the first place.

646
00:37:30,098 --> 00:37:33,930
Again, most games don't even have this limitation.

647
00:37:33,930 --> 00:37:38,200
It's not a perfect analogy, but imagine
a natural park.

648
00:37:38,200 --> 00:37:42,337
You may pay to go there as a service, and
there will be people maintaining the roads

649
00:37:42,337 --> 00:37:45,453
and trails, running a visitor center,
et cetera,

650
00:37:45,453 --> 00:37:47,531
and that takes real resources.

651
00:37:47,531 --> 00:37:50,231
But if the park runs out of money and shuts
down

652
00:37:50,239 --> 00:37:52,997
the park doesn't cease to exist.

653
00:37:52,997 --> 00:37:55,067
The trees are still there.

654
00:37:55,067 --> 00:37:58,104
The mountains and rocks are all still there.

655
00:37:58,104 --> 00:38:02,950
We don't erect an indestructable dome around
the park, that allows nothing in or out

656
00:38:02,950 --> 00:38:05,192
and can survive a direct missile strike

657
00:38:05,192 --> 00:38:09,086
and then tell everyone with a lifetime pass
that they can never enter the park again.

658
00:38:09,886 --> 00:38:14,275
But that's almost exactly how Games as a
Service works.

659
00:38:14,275 --> 00:38:16,135
This is not a good standard.

660
00:38:16,635 --> 00:38:18,777
I hope one of these analogies is sticking.

661
00:38:19,477 --> 00:38:24,232
Anyway the point to all that is even without
the law telling us what's what

662
00:38:24,232 --> 00:38:30,784
Games as a Service are really not services,
they're goods! So they should be treated as such!

663
00:38:30,784 --> 00:38:33,662
But hey, maybe you're saying they're a
weird exception

664
00:38:33,662 --> 00:38:36,201
and should still be treated as services.

665
00:38:36,201 --> 00:38:38,190
Okay. Why?

666
00:38:38,190 --> 00:38:43,674
I usually look at the law and rules from the
perspective of "what's the actual outcome?"

667
00:38:43,674 --> 00:38:46,163
Well the end outcome of most Games as a
Service

668
00:38:46,163 --> 00:38:49,734
is that they're destroyed, and can never
be played again.

669
00:38:49,734 --> 00:38:54,351
Geez that seems like a really high price
to pay to treat them as services.

670
00:38:54,351 --> 00:38:56,951
Now you might be thinking, "Well yeah
that sucks

671
00:38:56,951 --> 00:39:02,044
but then we also get anti cheat measures,
stat tracking, matchmaking, easy updates and

672
00:39:02,044 --> 00:39:03,544
[interruptive stuttering]

673
00:39:03,544 --> 00:39:05,685
See, that's the narrative talking.

674
00:39:05,685 --> 00:39:10,992
We can STILL HAVE all that stuff, AND,
NOT destroy the game!

675
00:39:10,992 --> 00:39:15,303
The narrative likes to frame it like you
can't have the good without the bad.

676
00:39:15,303 --> 00:39:19,160
That's what's known as a "false dichotomy,"
and it's just...wrong!

677
00:39:19,660 --> 00:39:24,123
It's like how you can simultaneously be
FOR the circus coming to town

678
00:39:24,123 --> 00:39:26,897
but AGAINST clowns stabbing people.

679
00:39:26,897 --> 00:39:31,570
I know that sounds impossible to some people,
but it's not that hard a concept.

680
00:39:31,570 --> 00:39:36,353
Companies don't have to give up ANYTHING
while they're supporting the game.

681
00:39:36,353 --> 00:39:40,850
It's only once they shut the game down that
they need to stop treating them like a service

682
00:39:40,850 --> 00:39:43,458
and give the customers their goods.

683
00:39:43,458 --> 00:39:47,359
Now you might be thinking "Yeah, but that's
probably a bunch of work right?"

684
00:39:47,359 --> 00:39:49,926
Well, it can be, but I'm not an expert.

685
00:39:49,926 --> 00:39:52,122
So I talked to someone who is.

686
00:39:52,122 --> 00:39:55,201
I asked that developer who worked on a
server emulator

687
00:39:55,201 --> 00:39:59,374
how much work would it take for companies
to provide the bare minimum

688
00:39:59,374 --> 00:40:02,146
to give users a reasonable chance

689
00:40:02,146 --> 00:40:05,926
to create a server emulator once the game
shuts down.

690
00:40:05,926 --> 00:40:09,128
Well, he said there were two basic options.

691
00:40:09,128 --> 00:40:12,204
I'm not even gonna go over these, but you
can see them here.

692
00:40:12,204 --> 00:40:15,218
Now this would NOT be enough for a playable
game.

693
00:40:15,218 --> 00:40:18,118
This would be more like those "repair
notes," like it was mentioned

694
00:40:18,118 --> 00:40:20,834
in those European planned obsolescence laws.

695
00:40:20,834 --> 00:40:23,353
But okay, this doesn't seem like too much
work

696
00:40:23,353 --> 00:40:26,698
so I asked him how long would it take the
developers to do this.

697
00:40:26,698 --> 00:40:31,428
He said it depended on how well the game was
written, and how competent the developers are

698
00:40:31,428 --> 00:40:35,204
but anywhere from less than an hour,
to a few days.

699
00:40:35,204 --> 00:40:38,231
Okay, so NOW let's compare.

700
00:40:38,231 --> 00:40:44,128
Here are the total pros and cons of having
Games as a Service, the way we do it now.

701
00:40:44,128 --> 00:40:48,909
Under pros, the developers save anywhere from
less than hour, to a few days work.

702
00:40:48,909 --> 00:40:50,617
Alright, that's a positive.

703
00:40:50,617 --> 00:40:52,850
And under cons, everyone who paid for the game

704
00:40:52,850 --> 00:40:57,380
along with anyone in the future interested in
the game, will never be able to run it again.

705
00:40:57,380 --> 00:40:58,525
Hmm.

706
00:40:58,525 --> 00:41:01,065
No this doesn't seem like a good idea to me.

707
00:41:01,065 --> 00:41:04,271
In fact, this looks like a really bad deal
for everyone.

708
00:41:04,271 --> 00:41:06,070
In order for this to be a good deal

709
00:41:06,070 --> 00:41:09,265
I'd say we need a situation like The Day
After Tomorrow

710
00:41:09,265 --> 00:41:12,908
where these people are hiding in a library
from the worst blizzard ever

711
00:41:12,908 --> 00:41:16,359
and need to burn some books so they stay
warm and don't die.

712
00:41:16,359 --> 00:41:20,180
See to me, THAT looks like a better
trade off for destroying media.

713
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:23,679
I'm pretty sure Games as a Service
doesn't save lives.

714
00:41:23,679 --> 00:41:26,956
Now I understand if you think I'm framing
this unfairly

715
00:41:26,956 --> 00:41:32,949
because it sounds kind of crazy that THIS is the
entire reason the industry is defrauding customers

716
00:41:32,949 --> 00:41:35,395
and destroying products with millions of
dollars

717
00:41:35,395 --> 00:41:38,105
and thousands of hours of work behind them.

718
00:41:38,105 --> 00:41:41,596
But this is the only honest reason I've seen.

719
00:41:41,596 --> 00:41:47,323
It reminds me how in the 60's, auto manufacturers
fought against seatbelts being mandatory

720
00:41:47,323 --> 00:41:51,613
because even though they saved lives, they
did cost some money

721
00:41:51,613 --> 00:41:53,668
and auto makers didn't want that.

722
00:41:53,668 --> 00:41:54,813
I won't lie

723
00:41:54,813 --> 00:41:58,223
THIS does cost SOME money to do.

724
00:41:58,223 --> 00:41:59,709
But not much at all.

725
00:41:59,709 --> 00:42:03,576
I would guess well under 1% of the total
budget.

726
00:42:03,576 --> 00:42:07,933
It's certainly cheaper than making a game
a service to begin with.

727
00:42:07,933 --> 00:42:09,715
So even without the law

728
00:42:09,715 --> 00:42:13,536
If THIS is the ENTIRE REASON games are not
preserved

729
00:42:13,536 --> 00:42:16,240
and customers can never use their products
again

730
00:42:16,240 --> 00:42:18,718
I don't find that particularly compelling.

731
00:42:18,718 --> 00:42:21,205
It's almost like if there are no laws on this

732
00:42:21,205 --> 00:42:23,005
then there SHOULD be.

733
00:42:23,005 --> 00:42:25,310
And that brings me to the final argument

734
00:42:25,310 --> 00:42:28,435
and this one I can even use on World of
Warcraft

735
00:42:28,435 --> 00:42:29,935
finally.

736
00:42:29,935 --> 00:42:33,734
Now this may sound crazy, considering the
first half of this video

737
00:42:33,734 --> 00:42:38,917
but I don't actually care about this topic
because the industry is breaking the law

738
00:42:38,917 --> 00:42:40,697
that's just a coincidence.

739
00:42:40,697 --> 00:42:45,010
I only covered that legal mess because there
could be some real power behind that.

740
00:42:45,010 --> 00:42:47,461
I think the law is going to be the only way
out of this

741
00:42:47,461 --> 00:42:50,011
but that's not my main motivation here.

742
00:42:50,011 --> 00:42:54,553
No, I care because Games as a Service
is destroying games.

743
00:42:54,553 --> 00:42:56,933
Quite successfully too.

744
00:42:56,933 --> 00:43:00,788
Every once in a while, you'll hear people
ask if games are art.

745
00:43:00,788 --> 00:43:02,453
I don't have an answer on that

746
00:43:02,453 --> 00:43:06,097
but, I think it's pretty clear that games
are creative experiences

747
00:43:06,097 --> 00:43:08,383
often worthy of preservation.

748
00:43:08,383 --> 00:43:10,677
So I'll say "art" just to keep it simple.

749
00:43:10,677 --> 00:43:13,063
I mean come on, if you're looking at stuff
like this

750
00:43:13,063 --> 00:43:16,688
and are saying "NO. This has no artistic
value at all"

751
00:43:16,688 --> 00:43:18,623
you're just being grumpy.

752
00:43:18,623 --> 00:43:21,720
You know David Bowie's in a game, right?

753
00:43:21,720 --> 00:43:24,379
Anyway the point here is that Games as a
Service

754
00:43:24,379 --> 00:43:27,351
is more than JUST a consumer rights issue.

755
00:43:27,351 --> 00:43:30,290
Even though the quality ratio is debatable

756
00:43:30,290 --> 00:43:34,110
games contain ideas, art, often dialogue

757
00:43:34,110 --> 00:43:37,754
and ways of interacting that aren't present
in any other medium.

758
00:43:37,754 --> 00:43:40,771
They're more than JUST products.

759
00:43:40,771 --> 00:43:43,674
If I buy a toaster that's designed to fail

760
00:43:44,422 --> 00:43:46,061
well, I still think that's wrong

761
00:43:46,061 --> 00:43:50,303
but I can always buy another toaster, and
still have toast.

762
00:43:50,303 --> 00:43:53,397
Whereas games are unique experiences.

763
00:43:53,397 --> 00:43:57,054
You can't replace them any more than you can
replace books in a library

764
00:43:57,054 --> 00:43:58,454
with different authors.

765
00:43:58,454 --> 00:44:01,735
You can't replace Dracula with Twilight

766
00:44:01,735 --> 00:44:05,310
and say nothing was lost because they're
both about vampires.

767
00:44:05,310 --> 00:44:07,116
It doesn't work that way.

768
00:44:07,116 --> 00:44:10,931
I'm not going to spend much time explaining
why saving art has value

769
00:44:10,931 --> 00:44:12,965
because for me, it's innate.

770
00:44:12,965 --> 00:44:16,898
If you don't understand why art or creative
works have value

771
00:44:16,898 --> 00:44:18,853
I probably can't reach you on that.

772
00:44:18,853 --> 00:44:21,196
Maybe a philosopher can field that one.

773
00:44:21,196 --> 00:44:24,361
For me, it's just an instinctive response.

774
00:44:24,361 --> 00:44:28,858
Now of course we can't save ALL artistic
works, that's just not practical.

775
00:44:28,858 --> 00:44:30,480
There's too many. Plus

776
00:44:30,480 --> 00:44:32,190
some of them suck.

777
00:44:32,190 --> 00:44:36,830
But we can decide for ourselves what's worth
saving, and what's not.

778
00:44:36,830 --> 00:44:41,154
Games as a Service denies that chance to
everyone.

779
00:44:41,154 --> 00:44:45,501
When I see a game with obvious creative
value being destroyed

780
00:44:45,501 --> 00:44:47,628
this is what comes to mind

781
00:44:54,692 --> 00:44:58,669
Now the reality is both better and worse
than that.

782
00:44:58,669 --> 00:45:01,490
The part that's better is that games that
are destroyed

783
00:45:01,490 --> 00:45:05,530
are far less likely to contain ideas that
are so powerful

784
00:45:05,530 --> 00:45:07,821
some see them as a threat.

785
00:45:07,821 --> 00:45:13,593
The part that's worse, is that publishers
are way more successful at destroying games

786
00:45:13,593 --> 00:45:17,133
than oppressors ever were at destroying
books.

787
00:45:17,133 --> 00:45:20,605
Or, you know, at least within the past few
thousand years.

788
00:45:20,605 --> 00:45:22,160
Even at book burning rallies

789
00:45:22,160 --> 00:45:26,951
people didn't burn every existing copy
of a book they hated.

790
00:45:26,951 --> 00:45:32,233
And yet, a lot of Games as a Service have
a 100% death rate.

791
00:45:32,233 --> 00:45:35,911
There is no comparison to that in modern
media.

792
00:45:35,911 --> 00:45:39,142
I guess in the early days of film we lost
a lot of stuff

793
00:45:39,142 --> 00:45:41,377
because the studios didn't know any better.

794
00:45:41,377 --> 00:45:42,879
That's what scares me.

795
00:45:42,879 --> 00:45:46,343
Now we DO know better, and yet we're still
going backwards.

796
00:45:46,343 --> 00:45:49,595
It's like watching a driver take a bus over
a cliff

797
00:45:49,595 --> 00:45:53,969
and he KNOWS he's taking it over the cliff.
It's a kind of madness.

798
00:45:53,969 --> 00:45:59,184
And speaking of modern media, you can rent
movies or stream music as a service

799
00:45:59,184 --> 00:46:00,865
but you can ALSO

800
00:46:00,865 --> 00:46:04,749
BUY THEM and KEEP them as GOODS.

801
00:46:04,749 --> 00:46:09,957
I guess some TV shows or radio shows you
can't buy, but guess what?

802
00:46:09,957 --> 00:46:12,190
You can back them up yourself.

803
00:46:12,190 --> 00:46:13,407
Legally!

804
00:46:13,407 --> 00:46:15,862
Not to dip back into the law again

805
00:46:17,123 --> 00:46:19,767
but that's what THIS case was all about.

806
00:46:19,767 --> 00:46:24,396
Technically, it's already legal to back up
Games as a Service is multiple countries.

807
00:46:24,396 --> 00:46:26,261
Even in the USA!

808
00:46:26,261 --> 00:46:30,820
But if game companies make that impossible
then what does that matter?

809
00:46:30,820 --> 00:46:34,215
I care about reality, not fantasy rights!

810
00:46:34,215 --> 00:46:38,835
I want to say Games as a Service are the
only form of modern media

811
00:46:38,835 --> 00:46:43,526
without real world limitations, that you
CANNOT PRESERVE.

812
00:46:43,526 --> 00:46:46,476
It's kind of amazing, in a terrible way.

813
00:46:46,476 --> 00:46:48,751
And that's what all this comes down to for
me.

814
00:46:48,751 --> 00:46:53,302
Even if Games as a Service passed every
legal test, which it hasn't

815
00:46:53,302 --> 00:46:55,121
then the law would be wrong.

816
00:46:55,121 --> 00:46:57,742
Games as a Service, the way we do it now

817
00:46:57,742 --> 00:47:01,911
permanently destroys games, and all the work
the creators put into them

818
00:47:01,911 --> 00:47:06,669
it's entirely preventable, and serves
almost no purpose for anyone.

819
00:47:06,669 --> 00:47:08,507
It's just wrong.

820
00:47:08,507 --> 00:47:12,931
The whole point of laws is to have a more
civilized society.

821
00:47:12,931 --> 00:47:17,886
Wanton destruction of art that others are
trying to preserve and have paid for

822
00:47:17,886 --> 00:47:19,907
is not civilized.

823
00:47:19,907 --> 00:47:23,295
That's why this needs to stop, even for
subscription games.

824
00:47:23,295 --> 00:47:24,734
We just have to stop it.

825
00:47:24,934 --> 00:47:25,695
["Stop it."]

826
00:47:25,772 --> 00:47:26,496
Stop it.

827
00:47:26,596 --> 00:47:29,019
["STOP IT! STOP IT! STOP IT! STOP IT!"]

828
00:47:31,393 --> 00:47:33,377
And that's my case!

829
00:47:33,377 --> 00:47:35,593
So I would be almost done, but

830
00:47:35,593 --> 00:47:37,404
but you know how the internet is.

831
00:47:37,404 --> 00:47:39,245
Somebody can make a good point

832
00:47:39,245 --> 00:47:41,934
but then somebody else comes along, nitpicks
something

833
00:47:41,934 --> 00:47:43,630
and then says "debunked"

834
00:47:43,630 --> 00:47:45,246
"all done".

835
00:47:45,246 --> 00:47:46,186
Uh uh.

836
00:47:46,186 --> 00:47:47,626
I mean that may still happen

837
00:47:47,626 --> 00:47:50,704
but, I want to wreck that before it even
gets started.

838
00:47:50,704 --> 00:47:52,983
Now if you're already onboard with what I'm
saying

839
00:47:52,983 --> 00:47:54,947
you can skip straight to the end.

840
00:47:54,947 --> 00:47:56,531
Yeah, see, there you go.

841
00:47:56,531 --> 00:47:59,933
But Games as a Service is a PLAGUE of
an idea

842
00:47:59,933 --> 00:48:03,772
so I need to burn out every last inch of it
I can.

843
00:48:03,772 --> 00:48:08,814
So I'm going into overtime to show just how
weak the opposition is.

844
00:48:08,814 --> 00:48:11,068
I think it's so bad it's indefensible.

845
00:48:11,068 --> 00:48:13,169
But hey, judge for yourself.

846
00:48:13,169 --> 00:48:17,997
So here are my rebuttals to every major
counterargument or concern I can think of

847
00:48:17,997 --> 00:48:19,749
BEFORE they happen.

848
00:48:19,749 --> 00:48:21,322
Here we go!

849
00:48:21,322 --> 00:48:23,279
Let's start with the big one.

850
00:48:23,279 --> 00:48:27,106
"Games as a Service is legal because you
agree to the terms stated"

851
00:48:27,106 --> 00:48:29,417
"in the End User License Agreement."

852
00:48:29,417 --> 00:48:31,142
Well, the simple answer is

853
00:48:31,142 --> 00:48:36,232
yes, those terms apply, as long as they don't
contradict anything the law says.

854
00:48:36,232 --> 00:48:40,689
If they say something that is not legal,
then those documents mean nothing.

855
00:48:40,689 --> 00:48:42,333
Nothing.

856
00:48:42,333 --> 00:48:45,412
That's why I spent half the video focusing
on the law

857
00:48:45,412 --> 00:48:48,152
and almost didn't care what those documents said.

858
00:48:48,152 --> 00:48:50,285
If the law says they're wrong

859
00:48:50,285 --> 00:48:53,125
that's all there is to it. It's not even
a debate.

860
00:48:53,125 --> 00:48:57,989
The end user license agreements are
irrelevant to this conversation.

861
00:48:57,989 --> 00:49:00,961
That's what that Lexmark Supreme Court
case was showing.

862
00:49:00,961 --> 00:49:03,819
They simply DON'T determine the law.

863
00:49:03,819 --> 00:49:06,694
It's good to think of those agreements like
"club rules."

864
00:49:06,694 --> 00:49:10,047
So if you're at the Moose Lodge and the rules
say you have to make an antler face

865
00:49:10,047 --> 00:49:12,127
before you can speak at the meetings

866
00:49:12,127 --> 00:49:14,266
well, then those are the club rules.

867
00:49:14,266 --> 00:49:18,159
You don't actually lose your right to speak.

868
00:49:18,159 --> 00:49:20,681
But let's crush this argument a little
further.

869
00:49:20,681 --> 00:49:22,611
Maybe you think the law is wrong

870
00:49:22,611 --> 00:49:26,089
and the customer assumed the risk of shutdown
when they bought the game.

871
00:49:26,089 --> 00:49:27,189
Okay.

872
00:49:27,189 --> 00:49:30,381
Well then when was it supposed to shut down?

873
00:49:30,381 --> 00:49:34,219
I don't think there's been one case in the
history of Games as a Service

874
00:49:34,219 --> 00:49:39,845
where the company told the customer WHEN the
game was gonna shut down upon release.

875
00:49:39,845 --> 00:49:42,660
That means the agreements are made in
bad faith

876
00:49:42,660 --> 00:49:47,653
because the company is purposefully
withholding information from the customer.

877
00:49:47,653 --> 00:49:51,730
Now personally, I think that's a weaker
argument that doesn't get to the core issue

878
00:49:51,730 --> 00:49:53,875
but it's still true.

879
00:49:53,875 --> 00:49:56,885
Game shutdowns are not a force of nature.

880
00:49:56,885 --> 00:50:00,059
They are entirely within the company's
control.

881
00:50:00,059 --> 00:50:03,051
In order to defend the end user license
agreement

882
00:50:03,051 --> 00:50:06,972
you would have to say that companies are
entitled to sell you a product

883
00:50:06,972 --> 00:50:10,085
break that product after the point of sale

884
00:50:10,085 --> 00:50:14,537
not tell customers when that's going to
happen, even though it's in their control

885
00:50:14,537 --> 00:50:19,307
and then, prevent customers from repairing
the product after it's broken.

886
00:50:19,307 --> 00:50:22,783
That is NOT good business, that is predatory.

887
00:50:22,783 --> 00:50:24,927
Good luck defending that.

888
00:50:24,932 --> 00:50:27,027
Okay, here's another big one.

889
00:50:27,027 --> 00:50:30,062
"Buying a game entitles you to the client
software"

890
00:50:30,062 --> 00:50:33,183
"you are not entitled to the server
software."

891
00:50:33,183 --> 00:50:35,886
Technically, yes, that is true.

892
00:50:35,886 --> 00:50:37,794
But that's not the whole story.

893
00:50:37,794 --> 00:50:40,517
Part of what I was trying to show in the
legal portion

894
00:50:40,517 --> 00:50:42,985
is that there's a precedent, if not laws

895
00:50:42,985 --> 00:50:46,948
that customers ARE entitled to a working
product.

896
00:50:46,948 --> 00:50:49,926
OR a reasonable chance of repairing one.

897
00:50:49,926 --> 00:50:53,633
So, no, the customer is not entitled to
the server software.

898
00:50:53,633 --> 00:50:56,772
But since they ARE entitled to a working
product

899
00:50:56,772 --> 00:51:00,071
that usually means the server software also.

900
00:51:00,071 --> 00:51:01,317
Doesn't have to!

901
00:51:01,317 --> 00:51:05,147
The company could patch the game for it to
run offline instead.

902
00:51:05,147 --> 00:51:09,543
In fact I have a really easy test, that sums
up my whole stance on this.

903
00:51:09,543 --> 00:51:12,073
Step one: Company sells the game.

904
00:51:12,073 --> 00:51:16,215
Step two: The company conducts business
any way they want to.

905
00:51:16,215 --> 00:51:18,829
Step three: Company support ends

906
00:51:18,829 --> 00:51:23,861
And step four: the customer has a reasonable
chance to run the game after shutdown.

907
00:51:23,861 --> 00:51:27,723
What the company does in step two here
is a "black box".

908
00:51:27,723 --> 00:51:31,782
I am in NO WAY trying to dictate how they
run their business there.

909
00:51:31,782 --> 00:51:34,116
I'm only concerned with step four here

910
00:51:34,116 --> 00:51:36,827
Can the customer run the game?

911
00:51:36,827 --> 00:51:40,524
In Games as a Service, the answer is
currently "NO".

912
00:51:40,524 --> 00:51:42,977
This is what it's all about, right here.

913
00:51:42,977 --> 00:51:46,159
Notice it doesn't say "release the server
software".

914
00:51:46,159 --> 00:51:50,314
It says give the customers a chance to
run their game that they paid for.

915
00:51:50,314 --> 00:51:52,970
HOW the company does that is up to them.

916
00:51:52,970 --> 00:51:56,650
In fact if you get confused by anything
I've said in this video

917
00:51:56,650 --> 00:51:59,282
and get lost somewhere, just look at that
chart.

918
00:51:59,282 --> 00:52:01,580
This chart is all you really need to know.

919
00:52:01,580 --> 00:52:03,409
Simple. Easy.

920
00:52:03,409 --> 00:52:05,423
I guess I should have put that at the
beginning.

921
00:52:05,952 --> 00:52:06,676
Oops.

922
00:52:07,358 --> 00:52:11,105
"What you're proposing would require businesses
to support their games forever."

923
00:52:11,105 --> 00:52:12,633
"That's unreasonable."

924
00:52:12,633 --> 00:52:13,769
No they wouldn't.

925
00:52:13,769 --> 00:52:19,485
In fact, I'm in favor of companies ending
support for a game ANY TIME THEY WANT.

926
00:52:19,485 --> 00:52:22,356
That's why an end of life plan is essential.

927
00:52:22,356 --> 00:52:25,314
If you're a company, and you end support for
your game

928
00:52:25,314 --> 00:52:29,450
but then the customer still has a reasonable
chance to play it after that?

929
00:52:29,450 --> 00:52:32,861
That's it! You're done! You don't have to
support anything!

930
00:52:32,861 --> 00:52:38,415
What needs to STOP, is support being a
REQUIREMENT to play the game.

931
00:52:38,415 --> 00:52:41,826
THAT'S what Games as a Service is.

932
00:52:41,826 --> 00:52:44,490
"If Games as a Service have to be treated
as goods"

933
00:52:44,490 --> 00:52:47,971
"it will hurt creativity of developers and
restrain them."

934
00:52:47,971 --> 00:52:52,222
Well first, I'd like to point out the
industry made games as goods for decades

935
00:52:52,222 --> 00:52:55,694
and I didn't see a lot of shortage of
creativity myself.

936
00:52:55,694 --> 00:52:59,099
[gibberish singing]

937
00:52:59,099 --> 00:53:02,955
And again, we're talking up to a few extra
days of work.

938
00:53:02,955 --> 00:53:05,364
I don't see how that would "kill creativity"

939
00:53:05,364 --> 00:53:08,316
unless the point of the game was for it
to die.

940
00:53:08,316 --> 00:53:11,940
Well wouldn't you know it, we've had a game
just like that

941
00:53:11,940 --> 00:53:13,214
"The Flock".

942
00:53:13,214 --> 00:53:17,393
This was a multiplayer game that the
developers actually wanted to die.

943
00:53:17,393 --> 00:53:21,209
After a fixed amount of player deaths, it
was going to shut down forever

944
00:53:21,209 --> 00:53:23,688
and they told people that right from the
beginning.

945
00:53:23,688 --> 00:53:26,705
Now I would actually allow an exemption for
this game

946
00:53:26,705 --> 00:53:31,121
because I think there's an argument to be
made that this was performance art.

947
00:53:31,121 --> 00:53:33,501
See, all other Games as a Service

948
00:53:33,501 --> 00:53:36,482
they don't die because the creators WANT
them to.

949
00:53:36,482 --> 00:53:41,199
It happens because they become unprofitable
and they handle the shutdowns irresponsibly.

950
00:53:41,199 --> 00:53:45,671
But this one, game death was the entire
point.

951
00:53:45,671 --> 00:53:47,308
So guess what happened?

952
00:53:47,308 --> 00:53:49,870
They ran out of money to pay for server
hosting

953
00:53:49,870 --> 00:53:52,868
so they shut the game down prematurely.

954
00:53:52,868 --> 00:53:55,910
So, the ONE GAME that wanted to die

955
00:53:55,910 --> 00:53:58,370
couldn't do it the way it intended.

956
00:53:58,370 --> 00:54:04,036
Its creative vision was compromised BECAUSE
of Games as a Service.

957
00:54:04,036 --> 00:54:10,013
Thus, as far as I know, games as a good has
NEVER hurt creativity.

958
00:54:10,013 --> 00:54:11,603
Let's look at that chart again.

959
00:54:11,603 --> 00:54:15,010
Does this chart look like it's strangling
creativity?

960
00:54:15,010 --> 00:54:17,020
But let's say it somehow was.

961
00:54:17,020 --> 00:54:22,553
Is that one game being allowed to die not
worth making rules for all others?

962
00:54:22,553 --> 00:54:23,873
I don't think so.

963
00:54:23,873 --> 00:54:26,744
"If the law was enforced on Games as a
Service"

964
00:54:26,744 --> 00:54:28,660
"it would negatively affect a lot of companies"

965
00:54:28,660 --> 00:54:33,296
"that would have to go back and change their
games. And they may not be able to do that."

966
00:54:33,296 --> 00:54:35,986
I consider the current situation so bad

967
00:54:35,986 --> 00:54:40,063
that I would accept that any laws on this,
not apply retroactively.

968
00:54:40,063 --> 00:54:44,914
If I can save all the games of the future for
the price of a hundred or so games today

969
00:54:44,914 --> 00:54:47,401
that's a devils bargain I can live with.

970
00:54:47,401 --> 00:54:50,762
Now it's not like I have any power to
compromise WITH.

971
00:54:50,762 --> 00:54:55,653
I'm just talking on a pure conceptual level.
I want every base covered.

972
00:54:55,653 --> 00:54:58,150
This is such a black and white issue to me.

973
00:54:58,150 --> 00:55:00,498
I don't want to leave any room for people
going

974
00:55:00,498 --> 00:55:03,895
"Oh, it's so hard to come up with a rule for
this."

975
00:55:03,895 --> 00:55:07,350
"I guess it's impossible to do anything
about it."

976
00:55:07,350 --> 00:55:10,598
No, I want clear rules for any scenario.

977
00:55:10,598 --> 00:55:14,666
In fact, if I miss one, come at me, I'll
straighten you out.

978
00:55:14,666 --> 00:55:17,881
"What about free or free to play games?"

979
00:55:17,881 --> 00:55:21,340
Well if a game is truly free, no money
involved

980
00:55:21,340 --> 00:55:26,158
I'd say the law doesn't apply, nor should
it. That would hurt creativity.

981
00:55:26,158 --> 00:55:30,979
People will want to experiment with concepts
and throw bad experiments out sometimes.

982
00:55:30,979 --> 00:55:33,543
There doesn't need to be a higher
responsibility on the person

983
00:55:33,543 --> 00:55:35,319
if they're just messing around.

984
00:55:35,319 --> 00:55:37,777
It's once they start taking money for that

985
00:55:37,777 --> 00:55:42,199
that they're entering legal territory, and
just a higher responsibility in general

986
00:55:42,199 --> 00:55:45,029
since then their actions start affecting
others.

987
00:55:45,029 --> 00:55:50,807
Now "free to play games," games that are free
but you can also buy extra items or features

988
00:55:50,807 --> 00:55:54,197
those are easy. Treat them like any other
commerical game.

989
00:55:54,197 --> 00:55:56,853
If you buy a virtual sword in a game

990
00:55:56,853 --> 00:56:00,278
then the game shuts down, so you can no
longer access it

991
00:56:00,278 --> 00:56:04,087
it's been taken from you, your property
rights have been infringed upon.

992
00:56:04,087 --> 00:56:09,300
Hence the need to treat it like the good it
is, so you can keep what you paid for.

993
00:56:09,300 --> 00:56:13,380
"Making a server emulator work on another
system is a lot of work."

994
00:56:13,380 --> 00:56:15,995
"Developers can't be expected to do that."

995
00:56:15,995 --> 00:56:19,510
Well, that's why I presented the "minimum
effort options" here.

996
00:56:19,510 --> 00:56:22,420
See, the situation is so bad right now

997
00:56:22,420 --> 00:56:25,295
I found a way to lower my standards even
futher.

998
00:56:25,295 --> 00:56:29,518
I used to think these were the only ways
for a company to not commit fraud

999
00:56:29,518 --> 00:56:31,365
for a Games as a Service shut down.

1000
00:56:31,365 --> 00:56:36,053
But wouldn't you know it, that minimum
effort solution sort of qualifies too.

1001
00:56:36,053 --> 00:56:41,351
If a developer provides THIS, then the game
enters "possible to repair" territory.

1002
00:56:41,351 --> 00:56:44,666
Which I still don't think is great, but, I
can live with that.

1003
00:56:44,666 --> 00:56:49,005
And again, this is anywhere from under an
hour, to a few days of work.

1004
00:56:49,005 --> 00:56:52,935
If that is still just too much work
for you to handle

1005
00:56:52,935 --> 00:56:56,261
then you shouldn't be selling Games as
a Service.

1006
00:56:56,261 --> 00:57:00,025
Laws enforcing games as goods would
negatively affect games

1007
00:57:00,025 --> 00:57:03,372
that get shutdown temporarily, but then
come back up.

1008
00:57:03,372 --> 00:57:08,817
Good point! I agree with you! That's why I
think a grace period is quite reasonable.

1009
00:57:08,817 --> 00:57:10,849
If a game is shut down for a couple months

1010
00:57:10,849 --> 00:57:15,203
but then comes right back up so buyers can
play it again, that's acceptable.

1011
00:57:15,203 --> 00:57:18,482
I think what the appropriate time period is,
is debateable

1012
00:57:18,482 --> 00:57:21,959
however, I consider "NEVER" an unacceptable
answer.

1013
00:57:21,959 --> 00:57:26,370
"Requiring companies to give players a chance
to play their games upon shutdown"

1014
00:57:26,370 --> 00:57:29,902
"is overreach, and an infringement on
business rights."

1015
00:57:29,902 --> 00:57:33,011
The whole idea behind a perpetual
software license

1016
00:57:33,011 --> 00:57:37,817
is the business is giving up a tiny bit of
its rights, in exchange for money.

1017
00:57:37,817 --> 00:57:41,859
It's the customer paying the business, not
the other way around.

1018
00:57:41,859 --> 00:57:45,246
What's actually happening is overreach
from the business.

1019
00:57:45,246 --> 00:57:48,711
They're trying to take back rights that
they SOLD.

1020
00:57:48,711 --> 00:57:53,643
If I were to sell you a car, and then later
steal it, and drive it back to my lot

1021
00:57:53,643 --> 00:57:56,282
we wouldn't say I'm "protecting my rights"

1022
00:57:56,282 --> 00:57:58,345
we would say I robbed you.

1023
00:57:58,345 --> 00:58:02,773
"You're just being an idealist. Things break
down or go bad all the time"

1024
00:58:02,773 --> 00:58:04,244
"games are no different."

1025
00:58:04,244 --> 00:58:05,403
Yes they are.

1026
00:58:05,403 --> 00:58:08,101
Video games are code. Numbers.

1027
00:58:08,101 --> 00:58:10,062
Games are more like stories.

1028
00:58:10,062 --> 00:58:13,892
BOOKS go bad, STORIES don't, unless we
let them.

1029
00:58:13,892 --> 00:58:16,354
Hey there's this story called The Odyssey.

1030
00:58:16,354 --> 00:58:19,296
It's pretty good, it's about 3,000 years old

1031
00:58:19,296 --> 00:58:22,051
though it's only been published for
2400 years.

1032
00:58:22,051 --> 00:58:24,563
When's that one supposed to go bad by?

1033
00:58:24,563 --> 00:58:28,553
But you know what, that's not a game, so
how about, Go?

1034
00:58:28,553 --> 00:58:32,298
That one's two and a half thousand
years old. People still play it.

1035
00:58:32,298 --> 00:58:35,359
So, no, this doesn't apply to games.

1036
00:58:35,359 --> 00:58:38,742
See, I have to point this out because some
people just don't get it.

1037
00:58:38,742 --> 00:58:42,415
Cheese goes bad, car tires go bad

1038
00:58:42,415 --> 00:58:45,097
math equations, do not.

1039
00:58:45,097 --> 00:58:48,686
Again, there's a real narrative pushing
back against this.

1040
00:58:48,686 --> 00:58:51,525
Just to pick on someone, take a look at
this article.

1041
00:58:51,525 --> 00:58:55,738
Eliot here argues that all these online
games are going to get shut down.

1042
00:58:55,738 --> 00:58:57,411
He's right about that.

1043
00:58:57,411 --> 00:59:00,089
Then, he tries to argue that this is
"welcome" and "inevitable".

1044
00:59:00,089 --> 00:59:02,185
Well, no, that's wrong.

1045
00:59:02,185 --> 00:59:06,525
He then compares game shutdowns to TV
shows ending, like Star Trek.

1046
00:59:06,525 --> 00:59:11,148
I'm guessing if he took a logic class in
school, he didn't get top marks.

1047
00:59:11,148 --> 00:59:13,219
See, here's the difference Eliot

1048
00:59:13,219 --> 00:59:17,986
people can still watch those TV shows today,
because they were preserved.

1049
00:59:17,986 --> 00:59:21,303
If no one today could ever watch Star Trek
again

1050
00:59:21,303 --> 00:59:24,338
then yes, that would be a solid analogy.

1051
00:59:24,338 --> 00:59:26,133
What he's actually arguing here

1052
00:59:26,133 --> 00:59:30,382
is development and support on a game
eventually ends.

1053
00:59:30,382 --> 00:59:34,319
YES, that's why an end of life plan is
necessary!

1054
00:59:34,319 --> 00:59:36,518
Their shutdown is inevitable

1055
00:59:36,518 --> 00:59:39,320
their destruction is NOT.

1056
00:59:39,320 --> 00:59:42,712
There are people playing Star Wars Galaxies
right now

1057
00:59:42,712 --> 00:59:46,826
even though that game was officially shut
down over seven years ago.

1058
00:59:46,826 --> 00:59:51,202
Do you think those people accepted the
ending back in 2011?

1059
00:59:51,202 --> 00:59:55,668
Now that's only possible because a developer
illegally leaked code to the game

1060
00:59:55,668 --> 00:59:59,608
but it just goes to show a game ending
is not inevitable at all.

1061
00:59:59,608 --> 01:00:04,708
Now I don't think Eliot here is a bad faith
actor, I think he just doesn't know any better

1062
01:00:04,708 --> 01:00:07,836
and it's unfortunate he's also a game
journalist

1063
01:00:07,836 --> 01:00:11,704
because then he influences others to buy
in to the misinformation.

1064
01:00:11,704 --> 01:00:15,253
This is exactly how a constructed narrative
works.

1065
01:00:15,253 --> 01:00:19,552
Go read The Odyssey Eliot, maybe something
will click in your brain.

1066
01:00:19,552 --> 01:00:21,471
"What you are proposing is outrageous"

1067
01:00:21,471 --> 01:00:24,671
"because it would require companies to give
up their intellectual property"

1068
01:00:24,671 --> 01:00:28,086
"and they cannot release their game without
losing their trademark."

1069
01:00:28,086 --> 01:00:30,780
Okay, I pointed out earlier that the
perpetual license

1070
01:00:30,780 --> 01:00:34,491
in no way entitles the buyers to the
intellectual property rights.

1071
01:00:34,491 --> 01:00:37,257
All they get is that single instance of it.

1072
01:00:37,257 --> 01:00:40,767
I was never arguing that rights holders
should lose their IP.

1073
01:00:40,767 --> 01:00:45,809
That's actually the whole point, people with
perpetual licenses have rights also.

1074
01:00:45,809 --> 01:00:46,999
As for trademark

1075
01:00:46,999 --> 01:00:51,325
companies can still protect their trademark,
and release an unsupported game.

1076
01:00:51,325 --> 01:00:54,779
They just have to be in front of it, and
define the terms.

1077
01:00:54,779 --> 01:00:58,620
As an example, Grand Theft Auto, a
commercial game

1078
01:00:58,620 --> 01:01:04,047
has been released for free, multiple times by
Rockstar, and they no longer support that game.

1079
01:01:04,047 --> 01:01:08,131
Do you honestly think Rockstar lost the
rights and trademark

1080
01:01:08,131 --> 01:01:12,089
to the Grand Theft Auto franchise because
of that?

1081
01:01:12,089 --> 01:01:15,557
I think they have some money invested into
that one.

1082
01:01:15,557 --> 01:01:21,300
Using trademark as an excuse to kill a game,
means the company is operating irresponsibly.

1083
01:01:21,300 --> 01:01:25,741
That's the point here, if the company wants
to solve a problem, they can

1084
01:01:25,741 --> 01:01:29,135
if they don't, there's infinite excuses.

1085
01:01:29,135 --> 01:01:32,918
Not good excuses, but, excuses nonetheless.

1086
01:01:32,918 --> 01:01:36,567
"You say games are goods, but old arcades
were services"

1087
01:01:36,567 --> 01:01:40,073
"so there IS a historical precedent for this!"

1088
01:01:40,073 --> 01:01:42,474
See, I knew you were gonna say that.

1089
01:01:42,474 --> 01:01:44,551
Well let's go back to that service list
again.

1090
01:01:44,551 --> 01:01:47,818
With arcades, you had one of these first
two criteria.

1091
01:01:47,818 --> 01:01:52,048
Maybe you played the game until you died, or
played until three minutes were up.

1092
01:01:52,048 --> 01:01:53,472
It depended on the game.

1093
01:01:53,472 --> 01:01:56,025
And for SURE arcades had real limits.

1094
01:01:56,025 --> 01:02:00,392
There were machines with space age
technology that weighed 300 pounds!

1095
01:02:00,392 --> 01:02:02,410
And hey, if you really wanted to

1096
01:02:02,410 --> 01:02:07,090
you could BUY the arcade machine as a GOOD
and preserve it!

1097
01:02:07,090 --> 01:02:11,802
Oh sure it costs thousands of dollars, but
that goes back to the real world limits.

1098
01:02:11,802 --> 01:02:14,116
It's just not the same thing at all.

1099
01:02:14,116 --> 01:02:16,881
In fact, arcade machines only PROVE the
point.

1100
01:02:16,881 --> 01:02:22,274
ARCADES mostly died off, but the GAMES
survived.

1101
01:02:22,274 --> 01:02:26,572
"You keep mentioning the law, and need for
law enforcement, but this isn't necessary."

1102
01:02:26,572 --> 01:02:30,031
"Companies just need to be convinced
preserving games is in their interest"

1103
01:02:30,031 --> 01:02:33,491
"and customers need to make conscientious
buying decisions."

1104
01:02:33,491 --> 01:02:37,055
Listen, we tried it your way already, it
didn't work!

1105
01:02:37,055 --> 01:02:40,880
For decades, this wasn't an issue for the
vast majority of games!

1106
01:02:40,880 --> 01:02:43,454
Nobody was enforcing laws on Games as a
Service

1107
01:02:43,454 --> 01:02:45,541
because almost nobody was doing it!

1108
01:02:45,541 --> 01:02:46,585
Great!

1109
01:02:46,585 --> 01:02:48,224
That's not what's happening now.

1110
01:02:48,224 --> 01:02:53,335
We're seeing large percentages of blockbuster
titles engaged in Games as a Service.

1111
01:02:53,335 --> 01:02:56,292
Why should we try to convince companies not
to do something

1112
01:02:56,292 --> 01:02:59,413
when they're the ones that created the
problem in the first place?

1113
01:02:59,413 --> 01:03:03,187
The companies that CAN be convinced, aren't
the ones doing this!

1114
01:03:03,187 --> 01:03:08,789
Remember, this absolute minimum effort
solution, could take under an HOUR!

1115
01:03:08,789 --> 01:03:13,061
But even I have to admit, that still takes
SOME effort.

1116
01:03:13,061 --> 01:03:16,367
Killing games requires NO effort.

1117
01:03:16,367 --> 01:03:20,260
I've heard people say before that companies
would be happy to preserve games

1118
01:03:20,260 --> 01:03:23,061
if they could just be convinced that it's
more profitable.

1119
01:03:23,061 --> 01:03:26,942
Well you know those people running shell
games on the street that are scams?

1120
01:03:26,942 --> 01:03:30,613
They're asking you to pick a cup, when the
ball is actually in their pocket?

1121
01:03:30,613 --> 01:03:33,117
I'm sure THEY would be happy to run an
honest game

1122
01:03:33,117 --> 01:03:36,752
if you could convince them that was more
profitable than scamming people.

1123
01:03:36,752 --> 01:03:41,416
But the thing about fraud, is that it already
has an excellent profit-to-work ratio.

1124
01:03:41,416 --> 01:03:46,548
A maximum one even. Customers are not going
to convince companies that honest business

1125
01:03:46,548 --> 01:03:50,409
is more profitable than fraud, because, it's
usually NOT.

1126
01:03:50,409 --> 01:03:54,151
But I think law enforcement could convince
them.

1127
01:03:54,151 --> 01:03:58,364
But hey, prove me wrong, maybe the industry
will see the error of its ways

1128
01:03:58,364 --> 01:04:01,557
and stop killing games before any of this
ever goes to court.

1129
01:04:01,557 --> 01:04:05,348
But do you really want to bet on the
industry policing itself?

1130
01:04:05,348 --> 01:04:08,014
Really? I'll bet against you.

1131
01:04:08,014 --> 01:04:10,964
Hit me up, my email's at the end of this
video.

1132
01:04:10,964 --> 01:04:14,088
"What about streaming only games?"

1133
01:04:14,088 --> 01:04:17,886
Streaming only games are ones you can
ONLY play through a video feed

1134
01:04:17,886 --> 01:04:19,402
it's the only way.

1135
01:04:19,402 --> 01:04:22,683
So forget everything that was shown in that
body and brain chart.

1136
01:04:22,683 --> 01:04:26,332
Streaming only games is like you're watching
all that through a window

1137
01:04:26,332 --> 01:04:28,455
You have NOTHING then.

1138
01:04:28,455 --> 01:04:32,570
At this point in time, these are mostly
prototypes, but, they're coming.

1139
01:04:32,570 --> 01:04:37,238
However, it's the same thing as before. If
they're under a subscription license

1140
01:04:37,238 --> 01:04:41,421
then, all I have is the preservation argument
not to destroy history.

1141
01:04:41,421 --> 01:04:43,540
I'm not sure there's any legal standing.

1142
01:04:43,540 --> 01:04:47,904
But if they're under perpetual licenses
and you BUY the streaming games?

1143
01:04:47,904 --> 01:04:50,019
Yep, back to fraud.

1144
01:04:50,019 --> 01:04:54,822
That's why we need to challenge this NOW,
BEFORE this becomes the norm.

1145
01:04:54,822 --> 01:04:57,967
And that also means they'll have to release a
LOT more than this

1146
01:04:57,967 --> 01:05:02,297
in order to let customers "repair" their game
after shutdown.

1147
01:05:02,297 --> 01:05:05,297
"You are pushing to enforce laws on games
as goods"

1148
01:05:05,297 --> 01:05:08,259
"but won't that lead to companies declaring
everything as a service"

1149
01:05:08,259 --> 01:05:11,146
"with a subscription fee and getting around
it that way?"

1150
01:05:11,146 --> 01:05:12,853
That IS a risk

1151
01:05:12,853 --> 01:05:16,807
but you know what, we've been there before,
and saw how it turned out.

1152
01:05:16,807 --> 01:05:22,362
In the early to mid 2000's, almost every MMO
game was subscription only.

1153
01:05:22,362 --> 01:05:24,093
Well guess what happened?

1154
01:05:24,093 --> 01:05:27,787
Almost all of them either failed, or went to
a different payment model.

1155
01:05:27,787 --> 01:05:31,184
Only a FEW survived on subscription fees.

1156
01:05:32,985 --> 01:05:37,211
So if every company goes to a subcription
model, most are going to fail

1157
01:05:37,211 --> 01:05:41,923
because gamers aren't going to be eager to
have five different game bills a month.

1158
01:05:41,923 --> 01:05:46,591
I think the economics of this aren't going to
work out for companies to do this exclusively

1159
01:05:46,591 --> 01:05:48,018
and they know it.

1160
01:05:48,018 --> 01:05:51,014
That's why Games as a Service exist.

1161
01:05:51,014 --> 01:05:52,775
They're pretending to be services

1162
01:05:52,775 --> 01:05:54,342
while they're really goods

1163
01:05:54,342 --> 01:05:57,821
and they're evading the responsibilities
of both!

1164
01:05:57,846 --> 01:06:00,503
"What about delisted games?"

1165
01:06:00,503 --> 01:06:05,101
Delisted games are games you can't buy
online anymore, or sometimes at all.

1166
01:06:05,101 --> 01:06:07,265
I have mixed feelings on those.

1167
01:06:07,265 --> 01:06:11,567
On one hand I of course want people to be able
to play games that they're interested in.

1168
01:06:11,567 --> 01:06:15,045
On the other hand I don't think it's reasonable
to force companies to sell their games

1169
01:06:15,045 --> 01:06:16,450
if they don't want to.

1170
01:06:16,450 --> 01:06:19,138
There's nothing fraudulent about what
they're doing, so

1171
01:06:19,138 --> 01:06:21,750
all I have is the preservation argument.

1172
01:06:21,750 --> 01:06:25,262
And again, this comes back to me wanting to
stay black and white on this issue

1173
01:06:25,262 --> 01:06:29,546
And I am LASER FOCUSED on not killing games.

1174
01:06:29,546 --> 01:06:33,464
Maybe once we solve that, I can care more
about other areas like this.

1175
01:06:33,464 --> 01:06:35,972
"What about games that are updated all the
time"

1176
01:06:35,972 --> 01:06:39,052
"so the early version is very different than
the later version?"

1177
01:06:39,052 --> 01:06:42,653
"Isn't it unreasonable for developers to
preserve each version?"

1178
01:06:42,653 --> 01:06:45,713
That COULD take a lot more work, yes.

1179
01:06:45,713 --> 01:06:48,661
Which is why I'm willing to compromise on
this too.

1180
01:06:48,661 --> 01:06:54,896
I would consider ANY working verison of the
game, as adequate compensation for the buyer.

1181
01:06:54,896 --> 01:06:58,291
Now of course it'd be nicer to have each
version available.

1182
01:06:58,291 --> 01:07:00,952
But again, I want to leave NO EXCUSES

1183
01:07:00,952 --> 01:07:04,291
not to provide customers with a working
copy of the game.

1184
01:07:04,291 --> 01:07:09,230
So if the company can only provide "1.0,"
and not "3.6," whatever.

1185
01:07:09,230 --> 01:07:11,462
ANYTHING, is better than NOTHING.

1186
01:07:11,462 --> 01:07:14,967
We're getting WAY TOO MUCH "nothing" right
now.

1187
01:07:14,967 --> 01:07:19,037
"I don't care if old games aren't preserved,
because I only play new games."

1188
01:07:19,037 --> 01:07:23,148
Fine. Then you shouldn't care if laws get
enforced on the old ones!

1189
01:07:23,148 --> 01:07:25,668
Although sometimes, they're not that old!

1190
01:07:25,668 --> 01:07:27,297
Anyway, this shouldn't affect you.

1191
01:07:27,297 --> 01:07:28,605
You can go back to sleep.

1192
01:07:29,444 --> 01:07:34,004
"This guy challenged EA in court on how his
game should last longer and lost"

1193
01:07:34,004 --> 01:07:36,653
"therefore you don't have rights on your
games."

1194
01:07:36,653 --> 01:07:40,801
This case was arguing the multiplayer portion
of his game should last longer

1195
01:07:40,801 --> 01:07:45,620
and it ended up being sent to arbitration,
which is notorious for siding with businesses

1196
01:07:45,620 --> 01:07:47,782
and the records were kept private.

1197
01:07:47,782 --> 01:07:50,780
He could have been arguing the case on
completely different grounds

1198
01:07:50,780 --> 01:07:53,777
than what I've been making that wouldn't
hold up in court.

1199
01:07:53,777 --> 01:07:58,227
We don't know what happened, and whatever
did happen, is not the law of the land.

1200
01:07:58,227 --> 01:08:02,966
This is also a perfect example of why legal
strategy matters.

1201
01:08:02,966 --> 01:08:06,843
If you go into the wrong court, with the
wrong argument, you lose!

1202
01:08:06,843 --> 01:08:10,864
"I found a ruling in the UK that says
perpetual licenses aren't perpetual"

1203
01:08:10,864 --> 01:08:12,264
"so you're wrong."

1204
01:08:12,264 --> 01:08:14,520
First, this was only in the UK.

1205
01:08:14,520 --> 01:08:18,674
Second, the perpetual part was more in
reference to its usage in the contract

1206
01:08:18,674 --> 01:08:21,708
and the fact that it was referring to 10
years to begin with

1207
01:08:21,708 --> 01:08:24,915
means it wasn't a perpetual contract in the
first place.

1208
01:08:24,915 --> 01:08:29,670
So that's just a really odd interpretation
that may not apply specifically to software.

1209
01:08:29,670 --> 01:08:34,764
Third, the European Union High Court ruling
on perpetual licenses supercedes this one.

1210
01:08:34,764 --> 01:08:39,732
Maybe Brexit will shake things up, but even
then, it will still just be the UK.

1211
01:08:39,732 --> 01:08:42,487
"If companies release information about
their servers"

1212
01:08:42,487 --> 01:08:46,329
"this means other games they're hosting with
the same software could be hacked."

1213
01:08:46,329 --> 01:08:48,113
I asked that developer about this

1214
01:08:48,113 --> 01:08:50,444
and he said in either of these scenarios

1215
01:08:50,444 --> 01:08:53,713
this doesn't give a hacker enough information
to hack another game

1216
01:08:53,713 --> 01:08:55,952
that he couldn't get without it anyway.

1217
01:08:55,952 --> 01:08:59,489
But even if he's wrong, I kind of want to
say "too bad."

1218
01:08:59,489 --> 01:09:03,924
At some point, a company should take
responsibility for its actions.

1219
01:09:03,924 --> 01:09:06,829
And that's how I feel about most of these
counterarguments.

1220
01:09:06,829 --> 01:09:08,417
They're excuses.

1221
01:09:08,417 --> 01:09:13,041
They're excuses not to do what everybody in
gaming should know is needed.

1222
01:09:13,041 --> 01:09:16,154
This right here, it NOT the end of the world.

1223
01:09:16,154 --> 01:09:20,289
Somehow, companies found a way to make
thousands of games in the past

1224
01:09:20,289 --> 01:09:22,194
and NOT destroy them.

1225
01:09:22,194 --> 01:09:24,910
It's necessary to go back to that.

1226
01:09:24,910 --> 01:09:26,414
And it's cheap!

1227
01:09:26,414 --> 01:09:30,086
See, companies are going to fight us all the
way on this, because, you know

1228
01:09:30,086 --> 01:09:31,795
customers are the enemy.

1229
01:09:31,795 --> 01:09:34,300
But THIS is what they care about.

1230
01:09:34,300 --> 01:09:36,579
THIS is their gravy train.

1231
01:09:36,579 --> 01:09:40,420
And preserving games doesn't need to
interfere with that!

1232
01:09:40,420 --> 01:09:43,411
If I could get companies to understand
ONE THING

1233
01:09:43,411 --> 01:09:47,401
it's that THIS is gonna be WAY CHEAPER
than any litigation.

1234
01:09:47,401 --> 01:09:51,062
I don't expect them to GET that message,
but I wanted to say it.

1235
01:09:51,062 --> 01:09:56,885
All I want here is the absolute BARE MINIMUM
of responsibility, to not kill games.

1236
01:09:56,885 --> 01:09:58,845
Just the tiniest bit

1237
01:09:58,845 --> 01:10:00,699
my GOD.

1238
01:10:00,699 --> 01:10:03,327
And finally we're done!

1239
01:10:03,327 --> 01:10:04,921
So what now?

1240
01:10:04,921 --> 01:10:07,888
Well, I didn't make this video for my health.

1241
01:10:07,888 --> 01:10:10,490
This isn't some TED Talk where I say
something positive

1242
01:10:10,490 --> 01:10:12,867
and then everybody acts like the problem's
solved.

1243
01:10:12,867 --> 01:10:15,960
No, I made this to actually get something
done.

1244
01:10:15,960 --> 01:10:20,243
At this point, I've taken things as far as
I can on my own

1245
01:10:20,243 --> 01:10:24,864
so this video is an SOS for HELP, to take
things further.

1246
01:10:24,864 --> 01:10:28,327
If you think you can help with ending this
practice, let me know.

1247
01:10:28,327 --> 01:10:29,793
Here's my email.

1248
01:10:29,793 --> 01:10:33,248
I probably need people like lawyers, or law
students

1249
01:10:33,248 --> 01:10:36,691
especially in consumer law, activist
organizers,

1250
01:10:36,691 --> 01:10:39,252
or anyone with connections to organizations

1251
01:10:39,252 --> 01:10:42,000
that could actually get the ball rolling
on this.

1252
01:10:42,000 --> 01:10:45,915
Here, I made cheat sheets of all the
important stuff I discussed in the video.

1253
01:10:45,915 --> 01:10:50,510
I'll make this, and key information you might
want available as a download link also.

1254
01:10:50,510 --> 01:10:53,472
I think there might be some meat on some of
the legal precedents

1255
01:10:53,472 --> 01:10:56,090
but we need them to be examined and ruled on.

1256
01:10:56,090 --> 01:10:59,703
Here's a few more ideas I had about possible
places to start.

1257
01:10:59,703 --> 01:11:04,431
And the thing to realize is, we don't need
to win all battles to win the war.

1258
01:11:04,431 --> 01:11:07,729
Say we can't make any progress at all in
the United States

1259
01:11:07,729 --> 01:11:11,154
but we can in the European Union, and
Australia.

1260
01:11:11,154 --> 01:11:14,952
Well that could be enough to make companies
add end of life plans globally

1261
01:11:14,952 --> 01:11:18,617
because then that will be cheaper than
facing fines or lawsuits.

1262
01:11:18,617 --> 01:11:21,185
Anyway, I don't know what to do with this
information

1263
01:11:21,185 --> 01:11:24,960
who to contact, what the procedure is, I'm
a layman.

1264
01:11:24,960 --> 01:11:28,010
It's a miracle I was able to put this
together as it is.

1265
01:11:28,010 --> 01:11:31,502
My most valuable function in all this, is as
a mascot

1266
01:11:31,502 --> 01:11:34,045
to get people fired up in the right
direction.

1267
01:11:34,045 --> 01:11:36,499
Yeah, like that! That could be me!

1268
01:11:38,093 --> 01:11:40,770
Somebody else needs to figure out how to
use this.

1269
01:11:40,770 --> 01:11:45,031
Maybe an agency says they could help, but
they need enough public interest first.

1270
01:11:45,031 --> 01:11:48,267
Well then I'll be right back up here
telling everybody to contact them.

1271
01:11:48,267 --> 01:11:51,127
Or, maybe all that's needed is some legal
person

1272
01:11:51,127 --> 01:11:54,091
submitting the right documents to the right
offices.

1273
01:11:54,091 --> 01:11:56,204
I'm trying to pull people out of the ether

1274
01:11:56,204 --> 01:11:59,922
who have more of a clue how to push this
forward than I do.

1275
01:11:59,922 --> 01:12:03,950
I said at the beginning that I'm declaring
war on Games as a Service.

1276
01:12:03,950 --> 01:12:06,579
That's true but this is retaliatory.

1277
01:12:06,579 --> 01:12:09,986
The games industry itself has declared a
war on ownership

1278
01:12:09,986 --> 01:12:12,360
against the consumer for years now.

1279
01:12:12,360 --> 01:12:13,805
And they're winning.

1280
01:12:13,805 --> 01:12:17,782
We're seeing the industry act with UTTER
IMPUNITY on this.

1281
01:12:17,782 --> 01:12:21,314
At the time of this video, a new era is
starting.

1282
01:12:21,314 --> 01:12:25,497
Stadia has been announced with
streaming-only exclusive games

1283
01:12:25,497 --> 01:12:29,014
meaning you'll never be able to preserve
those, it's impossible.

1284
01:12:29,014 --> 01:12:31,830
Not even that 0.1% chance.

1285
01:12:31,830 --> 01:12:33,298
And they're not alone.

1286
01:12:33,298 --> 01:12:38,728
So many companies are tripping over themselves
to do streaming games just like that.

1287
01:12:38,728 --> 01:12:44,037
The games industry is SO EAGER to take away
the concept of consumer ownership.

1288
01:12:44,037 --> 01:12:46,747
And that means these games are going to
get destroyed.

1289
01:12:46,747 --> 01:12:49,797
This is like something out of a science
fiction story to me

1290
01:12:49,797 --> 01:12:51,582
and yet, here we are!

1291
01:12:51,582 --> 01:12:54,704
Now I'm as cynical a person as you're going
to find on this topic

1292
01:12:54,704 --> 01:12:57,967
and even I am seeing a chance to change this.

1293
01:12:57,967 --> 01:13:01,386
The law could be our ally on this, we don't
know.

1294
01:13:01,386 --> 01:13:05,699
The problem is, the law has been UTTERLY
ASLEEP at the WHEEL

1295
01:13:05,699 --> 01:13:07,541
regarding the games industry

1296
01:13:07,541 --> 01:13:10,333
so we have to wake the law up.

1297
01:13:10,333 --> 01:13:13,186
We're gonna lose for sure if it keeps on
sleeping.

1298
01:13:13,186 --> 01:13:18,237
Even if it's against us, there is NO
DOWNSIDE to getting the law involved.

1299
01:13:18,237 --> 01:13:21,012
The situation has gotten THAT BAD.

1300
01:13:21,012 --> 01:13:21,777
Because of that

1301
01:13:21,777 --> 01:13:26,107
I'm only interested in plans where the end
game is some sort of legal process.

1302
01:13:26,107 --> 01:13:28,139
I think that's our only chance.

1303
01:13:28,139 --> 01:13:31,994
I don't think...changing consumer habits
will work.

1304
01:13:31,994 --> 01:13:35,300
In fact I saw a quote before that said
something like

1305
01:13:35,300 --> 01:13:39,504
"Anytime someone says the solution to a
problem is to 'vote with your wallet'"

1306
01:13:39,504 --> 01:13:41,790
"I know that cause is doomed."

1307
01:13:41,790 --> 01:13:44,936
That really reflects my own views as well.

1308
01:13:44,936 --> 01:13:47,422
I think legislation COULD work

1309
01:13:47,422 --> 01:13:50,295
but, I also see that as almost impossible.

1310
01:13:50,295 --> 01:13:51,918
I have no power.

1311
01:13:51,918 --> 01:13:56,496
If I was a multi-millionaire and could hire
lobbyists to push this through Congress

1312
01:13:56,496 --> 01:13:58,024
yeah that could work.

1313
01:13:58,024 --> 01:13:59,529
But, that's not me.

1314
01:14:00,223 --> 01:14:04,479
But getting judges SOMEWHERE to interpret
EXISTING laws?

1315
01:14:04,479 --> 01:14:07,046
That at least seems possible.

1316
01:14:07,046 --> 01:14:11,044
I care about games not being killed, but
I'm not gonna fight this forever

1317
01:14:11,044 --> 01:14:13,160
there are other things I'd rather be doing.

1318
01:14:13,160 --> 01:14:15,526
But I either need this practice to stop

1319
01:14:15,526 --> 01:14:18,179
or else I need to be DEFEATED.

1320
01:14:18,179 --> 01:14:20,146
I need to have high courts come down

1321
01:14:20,146 --> 01:14:24,702
and say games will NEVER be protected,
before I can give this up.

1322
01:14:24,702 --> 01:14:28,898
This is just "Phase One". Informing people
and the SOS for help.

1323
01:14:28,898 --> 01:14:31,630
"Phase Two" is to wake the law up.

1324
01:14:31,630 --> 01:14:34,149
We need knowledgable people for that part.

1325
01:14:34,149 --> 01:14:37,692
Now if you want to help, but don't have any
particular knowledge on this

1326
01:14:37,692 --> 01:14:39,431
well then you're in the same boat I am.

1327
01:14:39,431 --> 01:14:42,278
We're just gonna have to wait and see who
reaches out.

1328
01:14:42,278 --> 01:14:45,365
Believe me, I'll let you know if we need
your help.

1329
01:14:45,365 --> 01:14:47,504
In the meantime, the only advice I have

1330
01:14:47,504 --> 01:14:50,749
is making noise on this certainly won't
hurt.

1331
01:14:50,749 --> 01:14:54,897
Just getting gamers to understand that they
legally DO own their games

1332
01:14:54,897 --> 01:14:56,522
isn't a bad start.

1333
01:14:56,522 --> 01:14:59,450
I'm still impressed by that post explaining
it.

1334
01:14:59,450 --> 01:15:02,806
And you're welcome to use my chart. It's
nice and simple.

1335
01:15:02,806 --> 01:15:06,651
In fact you can take anything I've said in
this video, and use it however you want.

1336
01:15:06,651 --> 01:15:07,759
That's fine.

1337
01:15:07,759 --> 01:15:10,484
Maybe more noise will help, maybe it won't
matter

1338
01:15:10,484 --> 01:15:14,780
that's large scale zeitgeist manipulating
stuff, I don't know how to do that.

1339
01:15:14,780 --> 01:15:17,796
I'm single issue here. Stop killing games.

1340
01:15:17,796 --> 01:15:18,845
Stop it.

1341
01:15:18,845 --> 01:15:19,718
["STOP IT!"]

1342
01:15:19,718 --> 01:15:20,718
Stop it.

1343
01:15:20,718 --> 01:15:23,152
["STOP IT!"]

1344
01:15:23,152 --> 01:15:24,252
Stop it.

1345
01:15:24,252 --> 01:15:27,955
["STOP IT!"]

1346
01:15:28,155 --> 01:15:30,055
[music]

1347
01:15:30,055 --> 01:15:36,055
[Subtitles by Generic-User, quality control
editing courtesy of danielsangeo]

1348
01:15:48,805 --> 01:15:50,132
["This needs to stop!"]

